WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.348 align:middle line:84%
[SQUEAKING]
[RUSTLING] [CLICKING]

00:00:04.348 --> 00:00:10.420 align:middle line:90%


00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:12.922 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: So I know last
class was quite theoretical.

00:00:12.922 --> 00:00:14.380 align:middle line:84%
It was kind of on
the mathier side.

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:16.225 align:middle line:84%
So I hope people who
like math enjoyed that.

00:00:16.225 --> 00:00:17.600 align:middle line:84%
Today, the lecture
is going to be

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:20.005 align:middle line:84%
a bit more on the economic
side, so a little less math.

00:00:20.005 --> 00:00:21.380 align:middle line:84%
I mean, there'll
be some algebra,

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:23.780 align:middle line:84%
but it's a little more applied,
a little more practical.

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:26.140 align:middle line:84%
So today we're going
to talk about cartels.

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:29.360 align:middle line:90%


00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:33.580 align:middle line:84%
And I think it's helpful to
start with some examples.

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:37.480 align:middle line:84%
So the classic example of
a cartel would be OPEC.

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:40.800 align:middle line:84%
OPEC is the Organization of
Petroleum Exporting Countries.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:45.560 align:middle line:84%
And what OPEC does
is they get together,

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:49.200 align:middle line:84%
and they try to coordinate
the production of oil

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.440 align:middle line:84%
to ensure that oil
prices are high,

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:54.663 align:middle line:84%
and therefore, that all these
countries can make more money.

00:00:54.663 --> 00:00:56.080 align:middle line:84%
So the general
idea of the cartels

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:00.280 align:middle line:84%
we're going to look at today,
maybe I'll say the goal--

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:02.020 align:middle line:84%
there's many different
kinds of cartels.

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:04.478 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to talk about the
goal of these specific kinds

00:01:04.478 --> 00:01:05.519 align:middle line:90%
of cartel--

00:01:05.519 --> 00:01:16.800 align:middle line:84%
is generally to coordinate
on lower production

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:18.700 align:middle line:90%
in order to raise market prices.

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:29.480 align:middle line:90%


00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:32.760 align:middle line:84%
Do people know any other
examples of cartels

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:38.840 align:middle line:84%
that maybe you've come
across in your experience

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:42.020 align:middle line:84%
or in other econ classes, or
just things you've heard of?

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:50.920 align:middle line:90%


00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:52.220 align:middle line:90%
Any ideas?

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:52.720 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:53.700 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Are drug cartels--

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:54.658 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Drug cartels.

00:01:54.658 --> 00:01:56.400 align:middle line:90%
Great example.

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:05.040 align:middle line:84%
So drug cartels often
maybe interact a little bit

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:05.540 align:middle line:90%
differently.

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:07.040 align:middle line:84%
But they're certainly--
drug cartels

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:10.680 align:middle line:84%
are interested in controlling
what's on the market

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:13.060 align:middle line:84%
and controlling how
much revenue they get.

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:15.320 align:middle line:84%
Often, drug cartels
maybe operate--

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:18.040 align:middle line:84%
maybe the way that they limit
production or limit competition

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:20.060 align:middle line:84%
is often they
segment the market.

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:21.700 align:middle line:84%
So it's common for
one drug cartel

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:24.200 align:middle line:84%
to, say, operate in the north
of the country, another cartel

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:25.825 align:middle line:84%
to operate in the
south of the country.

00:02:25.825 --> 00:02:28.280 align:middle line:84%
And they have this agreement
that only one cartel is going

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:29.740 align:middle line:84%
to produce in one
side of the country,

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:31.900 align:middle line:84%
and only the other cartel will
produce in the other side.

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:33.710 align:middle line:84%
And that's one way of
reducing production

00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:35.960 align:middle line:84%
because you only have one
side selling in each market.

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:36.460 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:38.053 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: What if a
new group comes in?

00:02:38.053 --> 00:02:38.720 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Right.

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:41.480 align:middle line:90%
That can often lead to violence.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:43.660 align:middle line:84%
And even without new
groups coming in,

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:45.100 align:middle line:90%
there's often a fight over turf.

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:46.580 align:middle line:90%
So I like to use this example.

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:48.140 align:middle line:84%
This is a good
example of a cartel.

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:49.900 align:middle line:84%
It's not really going to
fit in with our models

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:52.525 align:middle line:84%
because our models aren't going
to have the threat of violence.

00:02:52.525 --> 00:02:55.000 align:middle line:84%
So I think this is an important
economic question, how

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:58.142 align:middle line:84%
criminal organizations use
violence to sustain cooperation.

00:02:58.142 --> 00:02:59.600 align:middle line:84%
But we're going to
think of cartels

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:02.380 align:middle line:84%
that threaten to produce
more of the good,

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:04.340 align:middle line:90%
not to kill the other cartels.

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:07.120 align:middle line:84%
So this has violence,
which will not really

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:09.440 align:middle line:84%
be covered in our
models, but it's

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:10.880 align:middle line:90%
a great example of a cartel.

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:15.220 align:middle line:84%
Other examples of cartels
that people have come across?

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:17.920 align:middle line:90%


00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:22.040 align:middle line:84%
So there's a famous one,
the famous vitamin cartel.

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:24.280 align:middle line:84%
In the US, these
producers of-- it's

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:28.560 align:middle line:84%
maybe not the sexiest
industry, but these firms that

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:31.120 align:middle line:84%
produced various
vitamins, they colluded

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:32.863 align:middle line:90%
to try to keep prices low.

00:03:32.863 --> 00:03:34.280 align:middle line:84%
I think they also
split up exactly

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:37.120 align:middle line:84%
which vitamins
they each produced.

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:40.240 align:middle line:84%
One that I think maybe
people don't think of as much

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:45.440 align:middle line:84%
that I think is a nice
example is actually

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:46.860 align:middle line:90%
the production of doctors.

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:50.105 align:middle line:90%


00:03:50.105 --> 00:03:52.480 align:middle line:84%
So I don't know if any of you
are applying to med school.

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:56.760 align:middle line:84%
The number of slots in the
entering classes at med schools

00:03:56.760 --> 00:03:58.760 align:middle line:90%
is very restricted.

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:02.760 align:middle line:84%
Medical schools cannot
enroll more students than

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:06.280 align:middle line:84%
they've agreed to with various
organizations, like the AMA.

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:10.960 align:middle line:84%
Why do you think doctors have
reached this agreement that med

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:13.728 align:middle line:84%
schools cannot enroll too many
students in the United States?

00:04:13.728 --> 00:04:15.020 align:middle line:90%
Why might they want to do that?

00:04:15.020 --> 00:04:15.500 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:16.800 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: To be able to
[INAUDIBLE] salaries.

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:17.550 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly.

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:19.560 align:middle line:90%
So it's exactly the same idea.

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:22.880 align:middle line:84%
OPEC wants to underproduce
oil so that the price of oil

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:23.960 align:middle line:90%
is high.

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:25.733 align:middle line:90%
How do we produce doctors?

00:04:25.733 --> 00:04:27.900 align:middle line:84%
Doctors are also something
that we produce and sell.

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:30.220 align:middle line:84%
I mean, it's a bit weird to
think about selling your labor,

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:31.220 align:middle line:90%
but that's what's happening.

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:33.240 align:middle line:84%
Well, the production of
doctors is med school.

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:35.365 align:middle line:84%
And if medical schools
reduce the number of doctors

00:04:35.365 --> 00:04:37.350 align:middle line:84%
they produce, then
the price you can

00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:39.600 align:middle line:84%
sell a doctor for, that is,
the wage that doctors get,

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:40.500 align:middle line:90%
will be higher.

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:42.780 align:middle line:84%
And that's exactly
what we see here.

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:45.520 align:middle line:84%
And classical guilds
also fall into this.

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:48.960 align:middle line:84%
Maybe some people would say that
the structure of PhD programs

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:51.680 align:middle line:84%
also fits into an
academic cartel.

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:54.840 align:middle line:84%
And then maybe one
last example I'll

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:58.040 align:middle line:84%
give that also doesn't quite
fit with our model today

00:04:58.040 --> 00:05:02.360 align:middle line:84%
is actually the Quebec
maple syrup cartel.

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:04.098 align:middle line:84%
Probably not well
known to people.

00:05:04.098 --> 00:05:05.640 align:middle line:84%
This one is different
because this is

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:07.640 align:middle line:90%
a government-sponsored cartel.

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:10.160 align:middle line:84%
So the government of Quebec
and the government of Canada

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:14.680 align:middle line:84%
explicitly allow this
cartel because they

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:21.280 align:middle line:84%
think that producing maple syrup
is a valuable cultural practice

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:22.920 align:middle line:84%
in Canada, and they
want to sustain

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:25.940 align:middle line:84%
the profits and the revenues
of producers of maple syrup.

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:31.620 align:middle line:84%
But because this is
explicit, it's contractual,

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:33.460 align:middle line:84%
it has explicit
government support,

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:36.960 align:middle line:84%
this also won't really fit
within our models today.

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:38.880 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to
think about models

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:42.440 align:middle line:84%
that have no violence, and
also no explicit government

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:43.060 align:middle line:90%
endorsement.

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:44.980 align:middle line:90%
So these are implicit cartels.

00:05:44.980 --> 00:05:47.320 align:middle line:84%
And I'll say more
about what that means.

00:05:47.320 --> 00:05:49.045 align:middle line:84%
So the goal of
these cartels tells

00:05:49.045 --> 00:05:50.920 align:middle line:84%
is to coordinate on
lower production in order

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:52.180 align:middle line:90%
to raise the market prices.

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:55.240 align:middle line:90%
What is the challenge?

00:05:55.240 --> 00:05:57.240 align:middle line:84%
Suppose you're an
oil-producing nation.

00:05:57.240 --> 00:05:59.400 align:middle line:84%
You get together with
the other oil producers,

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:00.852 align:middle line:90%
and you propose a plan.

00:06:00.852 --> 00:06:02.560 align:middle line:84%
We're each going to
produce less in order

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:03.960 align:middle line:90%
to keep the market price high.

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:04.940 align:middle line:90%
That sounds great.

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:06.060 align:middle line:90%
Everyone does better.

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:06.938 align:middle line:90%
The prices are high.

00:06:06.938 --> 00:06:07.980 align:middle line:90%
That's good for everyone.

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:09.063 align:middle line:90%
What's the challenge here?

00:06:09.063 --> 00:06:10.500 align:middle line:90%
Why might this not be so easy?

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:11.000 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:11.960 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Defectors.

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:12.793 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Defectors.

00:06:12.793 --> 00:06:14.560 align:middle line:90%
And how might they defect?

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:16.920 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: They start selling
more than they agreed to

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:19.660 align:middle line:84%
because the price is high,
so they make more profit.

00:06:19.660 --> 00:06:20.680 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly right.

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:22.860 align:middle line:84%
So collectively,
this is good for us.

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:25.160 align:middle line:84%
But the problem is that
each firm or each country

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:26.680 align:middle line:84%
will have an
individual incentive

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:28.720 align:middle line:90%
to deviate by overproducing.

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:39.360 align:middle line:84%
So the challenge is that
there's an individual incentive

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:41.987 align:middle line:90%
to overproduce.

00:06:41.987 --> 00:06:44.320 align:middle line:84%
And we can frame this actually
in the classical economic

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:45.880 align:middle line:90%
language of externalities.

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:49.360 align:middle line:84%
If a single firm unilaterally
deviates and overproduces,

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:51.680 align:middle line:84%
they impose an externality
on the other firms.

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:54.200 align:middle line:84%
What is the externality that
I impose on the other firms

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:56.640 align:middle line:90%
if I produce more?

00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:59.680 align:middle line:84%
How do I affect the other
firms when I produce more?

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:00.200 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:01.320 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Your
increased production

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:03.330 align:middle line:84%
leads to a lower price
than what they can sell.

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:04.080 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly.

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:06.760 align:middle line:84%
So when I raise my
production, I only

00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:09.247 align:middle line:84%
internalize the effect
it has on my profits.

00:07:09.247 --> 00:07:11.580 align:middle line:84%
But I'm actually hurting the
other members of the cartel

00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:14.560 align:middle line:84%
because by overproducing I'm
lowering the market price,

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:16.160 align:middle line:90%
and other people feel that.

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:19.377 align:middle line:84%
So this is a classic
issue of people

00:07:19.377 --> 00:07:20.960 align:middle line:84%
don't internalize
their externalities,

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:22.100 align:middle line:90%
and they overproduce.

00:07:22.100 --> 00:07:24.333 align:middle line:84%
You can go back to
pollution or congestion

00:07:24.333 --> 00:07:26.500 align:middle line:84%
on roads, all these classical
externality questions,

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:27.880 align:middle line:90%
this is what's going on.

00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:29.800 align:middle line:84%
So what's the
solution from the--

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:32.300 align:middle line:84%
I want to say solution from a
socially efficient standpoint,

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:36.200 align:middle line:84%
but a solution from
the cartel standpoint.

00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:38.920 align:middle line:84%
So I don't use solution
with necessarily positive

00:07:38.920 --> 00:07:40.400 align:middle line:90%
connotation.

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:41.820 align:middle line:90%
Well, there's a few solutions.

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:43.840 align:middle line:84%
One is to threaten
violence, but we're not

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:45.520 align:middle line:90%
going to talk about that today.

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:47.300 align:middle line:90%
One is to write contracts.

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:50.880 align:middle line:90%


00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:54.240 align:middle line:84%
What we can say is, we're going
to write a contract that this

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:57.140 align:middle line:84%
is exactly how much oil
you have to produce,

00:07:57.140 --> 00:07:59.320 align:middle line:84%
or this is exactly how
many units of vitamins

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:00.880 align:middle line:90%
you're allowed to produce.

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:03.185 align:middle line:84%
But often, we don't see
contracts written like this.

00:08:03.185 --> 00:08:04.060 align:middle line:90%
Does anyone know why?

00:08:04.060 --> 00:08:06.560 align:middle line:84%
Why do we not see
contracts as the solution

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:07.860 align:middle line:90%
to this kind of problem?

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:09.960 align:middle line:90%
I think there's a few reasons.

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:10.467 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:08:10.467 --> 00:08:12.300 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: I think it's
probably frowned upon,

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:14.760 align:middle line:90%
as [? making ?] [? a ?] cartel.

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:16.560 align:middle line:84%
Usually, the
government would want

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:19.580 align:middle line:84%
to force some kind
of fair competition.

00:08:19.580 --> 00:08:20.280 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Right.

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:22.113 align:middle line:84%
So it's not only frowned
upon, it's illegal.

00:08:22.113 --> 00:08:23.360 align:middle line:90%
It's very frowned upon.

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:25.280 align:middle line:84%
So in a domestic
context, I think there's

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:27.880 align:middle line:90%
two reasons we don't see this.

00:08:27.880 --> 00:08:29.500 align:middle line:90%
So maybe I'll say but--

00:08:29.500 --> 00:08:32.559 align:middle line:90%


00:08:32.559 --> 00:08:35.200 align:middle line:84%
maybe I'll start
a new board here.

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:38.080 align:middle line:90%
But what's the issue?

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:45.043 align:middle line:84%
Well, within a country,
this is often illegal.

00:08:45.043 --> 00:08:46.460 align:middle line:84%
I mean, it depends
on the country.

00:08:46.460 --> 00:08:47.918 align:middle line:84%
But certainly in
the United States,

00:08:47.918 --> 00:08:49.440 align:middle line:84%
if two firms get
together, and they

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:51.280 align:middle line:84%
were to write a
contract that says,

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:53.440 align:middle line:84%
we're only going to
produce this much, that

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:55.160 align:middle line:84%
would go against
antitrust regulations,

00:08:55.160 --> 00:08:58.518 align:middle line:90%
and they'd get sued.

00:08:58.518 --> 00:09:01.060 align:middle line:84%
What about-- but I think there's
slightly a different reason.

00:09:01.060 --> 00:09:02.920 align:middle line:90%
What about across countries?

00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:05.120 align:middle line:90%
So what about OPEC?

00:09:05.120 --> 00:09:07.320 align:middle line:84%
It's not necessarily
legal if Iran and Saudi

00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:10.200 align:middle line:90%
Arabia sign a contract.

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:14.960 align:middle line:84%
It's not really clear what
court would say that they

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:16.720 align:middle line:90%
were violating antitrust law.

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:18.400 align:middle line:84%
But I think there's
another problem.

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:20.960 align:middle line:84%
So why is it hard for
countries to write contracts

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:21.700 align:middle line:90%
with each other?

00:09:21.700 --> 00:09:24.520 align:middle line:90%


00:09:24.520 --> 00:09:25.020 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:25.160 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:09:25.160 --> 00:09:25.320 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE]

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:27.100 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Is it like
an enforcement issue?

00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:27.725 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Yeah.

00:09:27.725 --> 00:09:28.762 align:middle line:90%
So say more about that.

00:09:28.762 --> 00:09:30.220 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Like, you
have a contract.

00:09:30.220 --> 00:09:32.160 align:middle line:84%
Like, the only way
that you can enforce it

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:34.140 align:middle line:84%
is through your
own oil production.

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:36.880 align:middle line:84%
But like, there's knowing
you can snitch on and say

00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:38.177 align:middle line:90%
that they overproduced.

00:09:38.177 --> 00:09:40.260 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: So I think you're
getting at two things.

00:09:40.260 --> 00:09:41.802 align:middle line:84%
One is I think what
you're getting at

00:09:41.802 --> 00:09:43.600 align:middle line:84%
is kind of observability,
that maybe it's

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:44.927 align:middle line:90%
hard to see who's overproduced.

00:09:44.927 --> 00:09:47.260 align:middle line:84%
I guess I was putting at--
but your word "enforcement"--

00:09:47.260 --> 00:09:49.360 align:middle line:84%
I think one basic
question is, who enforces

00:09:49.360 --> 00:09:51.093 align:middle line:90%
contracts between countries?

00:09:51.093 --> 00:09:52.760 align:middle line:84%
If there's two firms
in the US, and they

00:09:52.760 --> 00:09:54.860 align:middle line:84%
write a contract, and someone
breaches the contract,

00:09:54.860 --> 00:09:56.200 align:middle line:90%
they can go to court in the US.

00:09:56.200 --> 00:09:58.620 align:middle line:84%
If Iran and Saudi Arabia
sign a contract, well,

00:09:58.620 --> 00:09:59.540 align:middle line:90%
who do they go to?

00:09:59.540 --> 00:10:01.620 align:middle line:84%
It's not clear who can
enforce that contract.

00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:04.680 align:middle line:84%
So across countries,
it's not clear

00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:11.880 align:middle line:84%
how we enforce contracts, kind
of no enforcement mechanism--

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:13.860 align:middle line:84%
no enforcement, maybe
I'll say, through courts.

00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:16.800 align:middle line:90%


00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:21.040 align:middle line:84%
So in both the domestic context,
where collusion as a cartel

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:23.440 align:middle line:84%
is illegal, and the
international context,

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:26.017 align:middle line:84%
where enforcing contracts
between countries

00:10:26.017 --> 00:10:27.600 align:middle line:84%
is challenging because
we don't really

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:30.400 align:middle line:84%
have a court to enforce
them, our initial solution

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:32.560 align:middle line:84%
of writing contracts
becomes a challenge.

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:34.480 align:middle line:84%
And because of that,
what we often turn

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:38.300 align:middle line:90%
to are implicit cartels.

00:10:38.300 --> 00:10:44.800 align:middle line:90%


00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:47.400 align:middle line:84%
So by an implicit cartel, I
mean we're not writing down

00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:50.620 align:middle line:84%
a contract that says, if
you produce this much,

00:10:50.620 --> 00:10:51.920 align:middle line:90%
this is what happens.

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:56.600 align:middle line:84%
But we're going to try to
arrange our behavior in such

00:10:56.600 --> 00:10:58.980 align:middle line:84%
a way that if someone
were to overproduce,

00:10:58.980 --> 00:11:02.000 align:middle line:84%
they would be punished, maybe
by the other firms producing

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:03.760 align:middle line:90%
more as well to punish them.

00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:06.840 align:middle line:84%
The problem is we
have to make sure

00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:08.760 align:middle line:90%
that this is self-enforcing.

00:11:08.760 --> 00:11:10.900 align:middle line:90%
But it must be--

00:11:10.900 --> 00:11:13.840 align:middle line:90%


00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:15.620 align:middle line:90%
maybe I'll say self-enforcing.

00:11:15.620 --> 00:11:19.280 align:middle line:90%


00:11:19.280 --> 00:11:21.760 align:middle line:84%
So the idea is we don't
have an external court

00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:24.700 align:middle line:84%
to take you to jail
if you violate it.

00:11:24.700 --> 00:11:28.017 align:middle line:84%
But now, well, if
someone overproduces,

00:11:28.017 --> 00:11:30.100 align:middle line:84%
then they're going to be
punished by another firm.

00:11:30.100 --> 00:11:31.560 align:middle line:84%
But then we have to make
sure, is that other firm

00:11:31.560 --> 00:11:33.143 align:middle line:84%
going to want to
actually punish them?

00:11:33.143 --> 00:11:34.980 align:middle line:84%
Are they going to
want to overproduce?

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:37.313 align:middle line:84%
And then we might need someone
to punish them and so on.

00:11:37.313 --> 00:11:40.480 align:middle line:84%
So what we want is exactly
a Nash equilibrium.

00:11:40.480 --> 00:11:42.987 align:middle line:84%
In the absence of
courts and contracts,

00:11:42.987 --> 00:11:45.320 align:middle line:84%
we want this agreement to be
self-enforcing in the sense

00:11:45.320 --> 00:11:47.900 align:middle line:84%
that no one can profitably
deviate from this.

00:11:47.900 --> 00:11:49.580 align:middle line:84%
So what have we
reduced the problem to?

00:11:49.580 --> 00:11:52.200 align:middle line:84%
Well, this is precisely
a repeated game

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:53.660 align:middle line:90%
between strategic players.

00:11:53.660 --> 00:11:55.560 align:middle line:84%
And we want to try to
understand the Nash

00:11:55.560 --> 00:11:57.153 align:middle line:84%
equilibria or the
subgame perfect Nash

00:11:57.153 --> 00:11:59.320 align:middle line:84%
equilibria of this game,
because that will precisely

00:11:59.320 --> 00:12:02.920 align:middle line:84%
describe what can be achieved
through a self-enforcing

00:12:02.920 --> 00:12:05.500 align:middle line:90%
interaction.

00:12:05.500 --> 00:12:09.820 align:middle line:84%
So today we're going to
look at a model of this,

00:12:09.820 --> 00:12:12.880 align:middle line:84%
which is going to be
repeated Cournot competition.

00:12:12.880 --> 00:12:19.240 align:middle line:84%
So formally, the model today
is going to be infinitely

00:12:19.240 --> 00:12:23.080 align:middle line:84%
repeated Cournot--
remember, Cournot

00:12:23.080 --> 00:12:24.940 align:middle line:84%
refers to quantity--
competition.

00:12:24.940 --> 00:12:32.760 align:middle line:90%


00:12:32.760 --> 00:12:34.580 align:middle line:84%
You can think about a
very abstract cartel,

00:12:34.580 --> 00:12:37.140 align:middle line:84%
but I think this is a
pretty good model for OPEC.

00:12:37.140 --> 00:12:40.000 align:middle line:84%
So keep that in mind as
you're running example

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:42.040 align:middle line:90%
as we go over this.

00:12:42.040 --> 00:12:45.220 align:middle line:84%
And we want to formally examine
the issues we've brought up.

00:12:45.220 --> 00:12:46.280 align:middle line:90%
We want to see--

00:12:46.280 --> 00:12:48.600 align:middle line:84%
we want to formalize
this intuitive idea

00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:50.280 align:middle line:84%
that firms will
have an incentive

00:12:50.280 --> 00:12:52.063 align:middle line:90%
to deviate and overproduce.

00:12:52.063 --> 00:12:53.480 align:middle line:84%
And then we're
going to see how we

00:12:53.480 --> 00:12:58.480 align:middle line:84%
can try to dampen that
incentive through future threats

00:12:58.480 --> 00:13:00.660 align:middle line:90%
to punish by overproduction.

00:13:00.660 --> 00:13:03.800 align:middle line:90%
So let's get into that.

00:13:03.800 --> 00:13:05.440 align:middle line:90%
So what's the model?

00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:07.280 align:middle line:84%
The model in each
period is just going

00:13:07.280 --> 00:13:10.060 align:middle line:84%
to be standard Cournot
quantity competition.

00:13:10.060 --> 00:13:17.040 align:middle line:84%
So let's say we have firms,
i equals 1 through n.

00:13:17.040 --> 00:13:19.600 align:middle line:90%
And we have periods.

00:13:19.600 --> 00:13:22.640 align:middle line:90%
Well, 0, 1, 2, 3, and so on.

00:13:22.640 --> 00:13:26.602 align:middle line:84%
Again, there's no natural point
in time when OPEC breaks down.

00:13:26.602 --> 00:13:28.060 align:middle line:84%
Maybe eventually
we run out of oil,

00:13:28.060 --> 00:13:29.500 align:middle line:84%
but we don't know
exactly when that is.

00:13:29.500 --> 00:13:31.167 align:middle line:84%
So I think the infinite
horizon model is

00:13:31.167 --> 00:13:33.000 align:middle line:90%
a pretty good model for that.

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:35.560 align:middle line:84%
And to simplify
things, we're going

00:13:35.560 --> 00:13:38.400 align:middle line:84%
to assume that each firm
has constant marginal cost

00:13:38.400 --> 00:13:39.480 align:middle line:90%
production.

00:13:39.480 --> 00:13:56.040 align:middle line:84%
So C, where we have
0 C less than 1.

00:13:56.040 --> 00:13:58.680 align:middle line:84%
This is 1.1 stylization
of the model.

00:13:58.680 --> 00:14:01.420 align:middle line:84%
In OPEC, not all firms
are probably symmetric.

00:14:01.420 --> 00:14:04.420 align:middle line:84%
Some countries have better
resources to produce oil.

00:14:04.420 --> 00:14:05.897 align:middle line:84%
They can produce
oil more cheaply.

00:14:05.897 --> 00:14:07.980 align:middle line:84%
You could extend the model
to an asymmetric model.

00:14:07.980 --> 00:14:09.120 align:middle line:84%
But to keep things
simple, we're going

00:14:09.120 --> 00:14:11.000 align:middle line:84%
to imagine that each
of the firms involved

00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:13.080 align:middle line:90%
has the same marginal cost.

00:14:13.080 --> 00:14:16.080 align:middle line:84%
And then demand is going
to be, as we always say,

00:14:16.080 --> 00:14:23.800 align:middle line:84%
the standard demand model, we'll
write P of Q equals the max of 1

00:14:23.800 --> 00:14:25.800 align:middle line:90%
minus Q and 0.

00:14:25.800 --> 00:14:28.880 align:middle line:84%
Remember here, P of
Q is the market price

00:14:28.880 --> 00:14:30.300 align:middle line:90%
that's faced by all the firms.

00:14:30.300 --> 00:14:32.840 align:middle line:84%
So this would be the
market price for oil.

00:14:32.840 --> 00:14:36.080 align:middle line:84%
The market price for oil depends
on the quantity of oil that's

00:14:36.080 --> 00:14:39.160 align:middle line:84%
on the market, and it's
decreasing in the quantity

00:14:39.160 --> 00:14:40.340 align:middle line:90%
of oil on the market.

00:14:40.340 --> 00:14:42.740 align:middle line:84%
The more oil is on the
market and is produced,

00:14:42.740 --> 00:14:44.200 align:middle line:90%
the lower the price is.

00:14:44.200 --> 00:14:45.860 align:middle line:90%
The price can never go negative.

00:14:45.860 --> 00:14:47.900 align:middle line:84%
So that's why we have
the max with zero.

00:14:47.900 --> 00:14:49.575 align:middle line:90%
So if tons of oil is produced--

00:14:49.575 --> 00:14:52.200 align:middle line:84%
well, there was a time recently
actually where the price of oil

00:14:52.200 --> 00:14:54.040 align:middle line:84%
got negative, but it
was only a little bit

00:14:54.040 --> 00:14:56.420 align:middle line:84%
negative because no one wanted
to store it during COVID.

00:14:56.420 --> 00:14:58.760 align:middle line:84%
But in general, the price
of oil is generally not

00:14:58.760 --> 00:15:00.080 align:middle line:90%
going to be negative.

00:15:00.080 --> 00:15:02.142 align:middle line:84%
And how do we
interpret this one--

00:15:02.142 --> 00:15:03.600 align:middle line:84%
I mean, one should
be like whatever

00:15:03.600 --> 00:15:05.520 align:middle line:84%
the total amount of
oil that could possibly

00:15:05.520 --> 00:15:07.060 align:middle line:90%
be produced in the world is.

00:15:07.060 --> 00:15:09.460 align:middle line:84%
So one is a very
big amount of oil.

00:15:09.460 --> 00:15:12.560 align:middle line:84%
We're just changing our units
to make that one unit of oil.

00:15:12.560 --> 00:15:18.522 align:middle line:84%
And Q, remember, is
going to be q1 plus qn.

00:15:18.522 --> 00:15:20.480 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to use
the notation from before,

00:15:20.480 --> 00:15:23.000 align:middle line:84%
that little q's
refer to how much is

00:15:23.000 --> 00:15:25.040 align:middle line:90%
produced by a single firm.

00:15:25.040 --> 00:15:28.000 align:middle line:84%
The big Q is the total amount
produced by all the firms

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:28.695 align:middle line:90%
together.

00:15:28.695 --> 00:15:30.320 align:middle line:84%
So the total amount
produced is the sum

00:15:30.320 --> 00:15:34.520 align:middle line:84%
of the amounts produced
by each of the n firms.

00:15:34.520 --> 00:15:37.980 align:middle line:84%
And then we're going to
use standard discounting.

00:15:37.980 --> 00:15:43.760 align:middle line:84%
So everyone's going to have
the same discount factor delta.

00:15:43.760 --> 00:15:46.720 align:middle line:90%


00:15:46.720 --> 00:15:49.360 align:middle line:90%
So what that means--

00:15:49.360 --> 00:15:52.460 align:middle line:84%
maybe let's try to
write this out formally.

00:15:52.460 --> 00:16:00.480 align:middle line:90%


00:16:00.480 --> 00:16:03.260 align:middle line:84%
So what I want to write down now
using this discount factor is,

00:16:03.260 --> 00:16:07.200 align:middle line:84%
let's suppose that this is the
sequence of production levels.

00:16:07.200 --> 00:16:08.960 align:middle line:90%
Now, there's no subscript here.

00:16:08.960 --> 00:16:12.880 align:middle line:84%
So what Q superscript 0 means
is the vector of quantities

00:16:12.880 --> 00:16:14.400 align:middle line:90%
produced in period 0.

00:16:14.400 --> 00:16:17.280 align:middle line:84%
So remember, superscripts
are always about time,

00:16:17.280 --> 00:16:19.360 align:middle line:84%
and subscripts are
always about the firm.

00:16:19.360 --> 00:16:20.960 align:middle line:84%
If there's no
subscript, this should

00:16:20.960 --> 00:16:22.920 align:middle line:84%
be interpreted as a
vector that tells me

00:16:22.920 --> 00:16:26.080 align:middle line:84%
how much every firm
produced in period 0.

00:16:26.080 --> 00:16:28.440 align:middle line:84%
Then q1 is another
vector that tells me

00:16:28.440 --> 00:16:30.600 align:middle line:84%
how much every firm
produced in period one.

00:16:30.600 --> 00:16:33.520 align:middle line:84%
And I want to compute
here what is firm i's

00:16:33.520 --> 00:16:35.720 align:middle line:84%
average discounted
profit if this

00:16:35.720 --> 00:16:37.960 align:middle line:84%
is the sequence of
production levels?

00:16:37.960 --> 00:16:39.640 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's going
to be a summation.

00:16:39.640 --> 00:16:42.380 align:middle line:84%
We're going to use 1 minus delta
up front, like we always do,

00:16:42.380 --> 00:16:44.640 align:middle line:84%
so that we look at average
discounted profits instead

00:16:44.640 --> 00:16:46.800 align:middle line:84%
of just the sum of
discounted profits.

00:16:46.800 --> 00:16:50.480 align:middle line:84%
And then we're going to take
a sum over all the periods, so

00:16:50.480 --> 00:16:53.000 align:middle line:90%
from t equals 0 to infinity.

00:16:53.000 --> 00:16:58.320 align:middle line:84%
And then we're going to have
delta to the t times the profit

00:16:58.320 --> 00:17:04.160 align:middle line:84%
that firm i makes in
period t, so times pi i.

00:17:04.160 --> 00:17:05.720 align:middle line:84%
Maybe this is little
pi i, and this

00:17:05.720 --> 00:17:09.160 align:middle line:84%
is big pi I. I don't know if you
can really tell the difference.

00:17:09.160 --> 00:17:13.280 align:middle line:84%
And here, we're
going to have qt.

00:17:13.280 --> 00:17:15.240 align:middle line:90%
Remember what is qt?

00:17:15.240 --> 00:17:20.000 align:middle line:90%
qt is q1t up to qnt.

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:23.500 align:middle line:84%
It specifies how much each
firm produces in period t.

00:17:23.500 --> 00:17:27.520 align:middle line:90%


00:17:27.520 --> 00:17:31.640 align:middle line:84%
So we can now say, what is a
firm's average discounted profit

00:17:31.640 --> 00:17:33.740 align:middle line:84%
from a sequence of
production decisions?

00:17:33.740 --> 00:17:35.480 align:middle line:84%
We can express it like
this, but we still

00:17:35.480 --> 00:17:37.720 align:middle line:84%
have to fill in what the
per period profit is.

00:17:37.720 --> 00:17:48.440 align:middle line:84%
So let's recall where, in
general, pi i of q1t up to qnt

00:17:48.440 --> 00:17:50.240 align:middle line:90%
is what?

00:17:50.240 --> 00:17:53.500 align:middle line:84%
Well, this says in period
t, what does firm i get?

00:17:53.500 --> 00:17:55.560 align:middle line:84%
Well, we need to,
as usual, keep track

00:17:55.560 --> 00:17:57.780 align:middle line:84%
of what is their
profit per unit,

00:17:57.780 --> 00:17:59.880 align:middle line:84%
and then how many
units do they produce?

00:17:59.880 --> 00:18:03.680 align:middle line:84%
So the number of units
they produce is qit.

00:18:03.680 --> 00:18:07.640 align:middle line:84%
In the t-th period, firm
i is producing qit units.

00:18:07.640 --> 00:18:10.280 align:middle line:84%
And their profit is going
to be that number of units

00:18:10.280 --> 00:18:14.240 align:middle line:84%
times the profit they're
getting per unit.

00:18:14.240 --> 00:18:19.504 align:middle line:84%
So what is the
profit per unit here?

00:18:19.504 --> 00:18:20.004 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:18:20.004 --> 00:18:21.879 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: [? Will ?]
[INAUDIBLE] get the price

00:18:21.879 --> 00:18:23.810 align:middle line:84%
by summing all the
quantities at this time.

00:18:23.810 --> 00:18:24.560 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly.

00:18:24.560 --> 00:18:26.500 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: And then use pi C.

00:18:26.500 --> 00:18:27.500 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly right.

00:18:27.500 --> 00:18:32.920 align:middle line:90%
So that's going to be P of q1t--

00:18:32.920 --> 00:18:34.400 align:middle line:90%
try to write small here--

00:18:34.400 --> 00:18:35.680 align:middle line:90%
up to qnt.

00:18:35.680 --> 00:18:37.080 align:middle line:84%
This is the sum
of the quantities

00:18:37.080 --> 00:18:38.460 align:middle line:90%
that are produced at time t.

00:18:38.460 --> 00:18:40.027 align:middle line:90%
So that's the market quantity.

00:18:40.027 --> 00:18:41.360 align:middle line:90%
That's the amount in the market.

00:18:41.360 --> 00:18:43.088 align:middle line:84%
That determines the
market price, P.

00:18:43.088 --> 00:18:44.880 align:middle line:84%
But of course, that's
not all profit for me

00:18:44.880 --> 00:18:47.460 align:middle line:84%
because I also have to
pay a cost of production.

00:18:47.460 --> 00:18:48.620 align:middle line:90%
So we subtract C here.

00:18:48.620 --> 00:18:57.280 align:middle line:90%


00:18:57.280 --> 00:19:02.760 align:middle line:84%
So now we've
formalized the game.

00:19:02.760 --> 00:19:04.280 align:middle line:84%
I guess one final
assumption we have

00:19:04.280 --> 00:19:06.447 align:middle line:84%
to keep track of is remember,
whenever we've studied

00:19:06.447 --> 00:19:08.600 align:middle line:84%
infinitely repeated
games, we've assumed

00:19:08.600 --> 00:19:10.332 align:middle line:90%
that past actions are observed.

00:19:10.332 --> 00:19:12.540 align:middle line:84%
And in this context, that
may be a strong assumption,

00:19:12.540 --> 00:19:13.940 align:middle line:84%
and this goes to
a question we had.

00:19:13.940 --> 00:19:15.982 align:middle line:84%
Remember, we're going to
maintain the assumption.

00:19:15.982 --> 00:19:30.760 align:middle line:84%
So I'll say
maintained assumption

00:19:30.760 --> 00:19:32.880 align:middle line:84%
past actions are observed,
which in this case,

00:19:32.880 --> 00:19:36.880 align:middle line:84%
past actions are just
quantities produced.

00:19:36.880 --> 00:19:42.880 align:middle line:84%
Do you think this is a good
assumption in this model or not?

00:19:42.880 --> 00:19:43.480 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:19:43.480 --> 00:19:43.980 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:19:43.980 --> 00:19:45.518 align:middle line:90%
I think it's not great.

00:19:45.518 --> 00:19:46.060 align:middle line:90%
Not terrible.

00:19:46.060 --> 00:19:47.680 align:middle line:90%
It depends on the context.

00:19:47.680 --> 00:19:49.530 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes we might
be able to see

00:19:49.530 --> 00:19:50.780 align:middle line:90%
how much people are producing.

00:19:50.780 --> 00:19:52.360 align:middle line:90%
Sometimes it may be a bit hard.

00:19:52.360 --> 00:19:54.280 align:middle line:84%
And if you read when
OPEC has disagreements,

00:19:54.280 --> 00:19:55.580 align:middle line:84%
this is always
what happens right.

00:19:55.580 --> 00:19:57.080 align:middle line:84%
One country says, oh, you
definitely overproduced.

00:19:57.080 --> 00:19:57.830 align:middle line:90%
They said, oh, no.

00:19:57.830 --> 00:19:58.600 align:middle line:90%
It wasn't me.

00:19:58.600 --> 00:19:59.600 align:middle line:90%
And you'll see cartels--

00:19:59.600 --> 00:20:02.058 align:middle line:84%
I mean, if you watched Breaking
Bad, this is often a story.

00:20:02.058 --> 00:20:04.080 align:middle line:84%
People are trying to
secretly sell their product

00:20:04.080 --> 00:20:06.160 align:middle line:84%
without other members
of the cartel observing

00:20:06.160 --> 00:20:09.000 align:middle line:90%
that they're selling it.

00:20:09.000 --> 00:20:11.560 align:middle line:84%
And I think maybe a
better model of this

00:20:11.560 --> 00:20:14.400 align:middle line:84%
would be that we observe noisy
signals of how much people

00:20:14.400 --> 00:20:15.060 align:middle line:90%
have produced.

00:20:15.060 --> 00:20:17.200 align:middle line:84%
Because really, what we
observe is the price.

00:20:17.200 --> 00:20:18.880 align:middle line:84%
And what happens
with OPEC is they see

00:20:18.880 --> 00:20:20.100 align:middle line:90%
the market price has dropped.

00:20:20.100 --> 00:20:21.960 align:middle line:84%
Everyone sees that,
and that suggests

00:20:21.960 --> 00:20:23.520 align:middle line:90%
that someone has overproduced.

00:20:23.520 --> 00:20:26.160 align:middle line:84%
The hard thing is to figure out
who it was who overproduced.

00:20:26.160 --> 00:20:28.520 align:middle line:84%
And that's an
exciting topic, kind

00:20:28.520 --> 00:20:30.540 align:middle line:84%
of getting more
modern research ideas,

00:20:30.540 --> 00:20:32.680 align:middle line:84%
what we do when we only
have imperfect monitoring

00:20:32.680 --> 00:20:33.700 align:middle line:90%
of past quantities.

00:20:33.700 --> 00:20:35.860 align:middle line:84%
Unfortunately, it's
mathematically a lot harder,

00:20:35.860 --> 00:20:36.940 align:middle line:84%
so we won't cover
it in this class.

00:20:36.940 --> 00:20:38.460 align:middle line:84%
But if you continue
studying game theory,

00:20:38.460 --> 00:20:39.660 align:middle line:84%
I think that's a really
interesting topic.

00:20:39.660 --> 00:20:42.143 align:middle line:84%
What happens if we see price
as gone-- is really low,

00:20:42.143 --> 00:20:43.560 align:middle line:84%
and we're trying
to figure out who

00:20:43.560 --> 00:20:45.180 align:middle line:84%
it was who did the
overproduction,

00:20:45.180 --> 00:20:46.920 align:middle line:84%
and we don't really
know who it is.

00:20:46.920 --> 00:20:50.280 align:middle line:84%
But we'll maintain
that assumption today.

00:20:50.280 --> 00:20:52.840 align:middle line:84%
So before we get
into actually solving

00:20:52.840 --> 00:20:55.860 align:middle line:84%
for a subgame perfect Nash
equilibrium of this game,

00:20:55.860 --> 00:20:59.880 align:middle line:84%
let's try to just think
about this basic challenge

00:20:59.880 --> 00:21:02.180 align:middle line:84%
of each firm having an
incentive to overproduce.

00:21:02.180 --> 00:21:04.400 align:middle line:84%
So let's just first
think of the static--

00:21:04.400 --> 00:21:06.140 align:middle line:84%
let's just first look
at the stage game.

00:21:06.140 --> 00:21:09.120 align:middle line:90%


00:21:09.120 --> 00:21:11.600 align:middle line:84%
And let's see if
we can remember--

00:21:11.600 --> 00:21:14.540 align:middle line:84%
the stage game has a unique Nash
equilibrium that we solve for.

00:21:14.540 --> 00:21:18.240 align:middle line:84%
I think this is a good thing to
keep in mind maybe for exams.

00:21:18.240 --> 00:21:24.412 align:middle line:84%
The stage game Nash equilibrium
maybe I'll say was q1 equals qn.

00:21:24.412 --> 00:21:26.620 align:middle line:84%
Remember, now, we're just
thinking of the stage game.

00:21:26.620 --> 00:21:28.495 align:middle line:84%
So let's just think
about everyone's choosing

00:21:28.495 --> 00:21:31.240 align:middle line:84%
how much to produce, and
everyone produced maybe

00:21:31.240 --> 00:21:34.040 align:middle line:90%
what I'll call qNE.

00:21:34.040 --> 00:21:36.220 align:middle line:84%
This is the Nash
equilibrium quantity.

00:21:36.220 --> 00:21:38.280 align:middle line:90%
So this is a number.

00:21:38.280 --> 00:21:43.880 align:middle line:84%
And that number turned out to
be 1 minus c over n plus 1.

00:21:43.880 --> 00:21:46.772 align:middle line:84%
So it turned out
that in equilibrium,

00:21:46.772 --> 00:21:48.480 align:middle line:84%
the amount that everyone
produced-- well,

00:21:48.480 --> 00:21:50.280 align:middle line:84%
it depends on how
many firms there are.

00:21:50.280 --> 00:21:52.300 align:middle line:84%
The more firms there
are in the market,

00:21:52.300 --> 00:21:54.920 align:middle line:84%
the less each firm
produces, and the higher

00:21:54.920 --> 00:21:59.400 align:middle line:84%
the cost c of production,
the less each firm produces.

00:21:59.400 --> 00:22:06.840 align:middle line:84%
And what that meant was the
market price, pNE, was what?

00:22:06.840 --> 00:22:10.340 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's 1 minus
the total quantity.

00:22:10.340 --> 00:22:12.920 align:middle line:84%
So maybe let's
keep track of this.

00:22:12.920 --> 00:22:15.040 align:middle line:90%
What is qNE?

00:22:15.040 --> 00:22:17.880 align:middle line:84%
So I've told you what each
individual firm produces.

00:22:17.880 --> 00:22:20.293 align:middle line:84%
qNE is the aggregate
quantity produced.

00:22:20.293 --> 00:22:21.960 align:middle line:84%
Well, that's just the
sum of the amounts

00:22:21.960 --> 00:22:24.140 align:middle line:84%
that the individual
firms produce.

00:22:24.140 --> 00:22:28.180 align:middle line:84%
So I have 1 minus c over n
plus 1 summed over the n firms.

00:22:28.180 --> 00:22:28.900 align:middle line:90%
What is that?

00:22:28.900 --> 00:22:33.400 align:middle line:84%
That's just n over n
plus 1 times 1 minus c.

00:22:33.400 --> 00:22:36.080 align:middle line:84%
I've just multiplied this
individual production level

00:22:36.080 --> 00:22:38.800 align:middle line:90%
by the total number of firms, n.

00:22:38.800 --> 00:22:41.820 align:middle line:84%
And once we see that, we
can see the market price,

00:22:41.820 --> 00:22:58.240 align:middle line:84%
pNE, is going to be 1 minus
qNE, which is 1 minus n

00:22:58.240 --> 00:23:02.520 align:middle line:84%
over plus n plus
1 times 1 minus c.

00:23:02.520 --> 00:23:03.818 align:middle line:90%
And there's a nice--

00:23:03.818 --> 00:23:05.360 align:middle line:84%
just to help us
understand this, this

00:23:05.360 --> 00:23:09.280 align:middle line:84%
is kind of a convenient
algebra trick.

00:23:09.280 --> 00:23:12.440 align:middle line:90%
Let's add c and subtract c.

00:23:12.440 --> 00:23:15.680 align:middle line:84%
So if we do that, I'll
add c to the front.

00:23:15.680 --> 00:23:17.460 align:middle line:84%
And now I'm going to
subtract c later on.

00:23:17.460 --> 00:23:24.600 align:middle line:84%
So we get 1 minus c minus
n over n plus 1, 1 minus c.

00:23:24.600 --> 00:23:27.640 align:middle line:84%
So all I've done is
I've put in a c here,

00:23:27.640 --> 00:23:29.840 align:middle line:90%
and I've subtracted a c here.

00:23:29.840 --> 00:23:32.080 align:middle line:90%
So I haven't done anything.

00:23:32.080 --> 00:23:34.240 align:middle line:84%
But now we can
simplify things a lot

00:23:34.240 --> 00:23:38.720 align:middle line:84%
because I have 1 minus c minus
n over n plus 1 times 1 minus c.

00:23:38.720 --> 00:23:44.360 align:middle line:84%
So if I factor out the 1
minus c, I get c plus 1

00:23:44.360 --> 00:23:46.340 align:middle line:90%
minus c over n plus 1.

00:23:46.340 --> 00:23:55.000 align:middle line:90%


00:23:55.000 --> 00:23:57.820 align:middle line:84%
And I think this is a bit
more easily interpretable.

00:23:57.820 --> 00:24:00.920 align:middle line:84%
Can people-- does anyone want
to give an interpretation

00:24:00.920 --> 00:24:03.360 align:middle line:90%
for this market price here?

00:24:03.360 --> 00:24:07.400 align:middle line:84%
So how much is each firm
charging in this equilibrium?

00:24:07.400 --> 00:24:09.680 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I'll just go--
they're charging-- well,

00:24:09.680 --> 00:24:11.740 align:middle line:90%
their cost plus a markup.

00:24:11.740 --> 00:24:15.760 align:middle line:84%
So this is the
classic markup term.

00:24:15.760 --> 00:24:18.980 align:middle line:84%
This tells me that each firm
is making positive profits

00:24:18.980 --> 00:24:21.640 align:middle line:84%
because each firm is
producing a positive amount,

00:24:21.640 --> 00:24:23.720 align:middle line:84%
and the price they're
getting is strictly higher

00:24:23.720 --> 00:24:25.140 align:middle line:90%
than the cost of production.

00:24:25.140 --> 00:24:28.120 align:middle line:84%
So that means their profit
per unit is strictly positive.

00:24:28.120 --> 00:24:30.480 align:middle line:84%
And the scale of the
markup is decreasing

00:24:30.480 --> 00:24:31.580 align:middle line:90%
in the number of firms.

00:24:31.580 --> 00:24:34.020 align:middle line:84%
The more firms there are,
the smaller this markup is.

00:24:34.020 --> 00:24:37.930 align:middle line:90%


00:24:37.930 --> 00:24:41.080 align:middle line:84%
And now, let's just
compute one last thing,

00:24:41.080 --> 00:24:44.920 align:middle line:90%
which is what is pi NE?

00:24:44.920 --> 00:24:51.600 align:middle line:84%
So what is the profit that each
firm makes in this equilibrium?

00:24:51.600 --> 00:24:55.720 align:middle line:84%
Well, each firm is producing
1 minus c over n plus 1.

00:24:55.720 --> 00:24:57.640 align:middle line:84%
And their profit
per unit is exactly

00:24:57.640 --> 00:25:00.600 align:middle line:84%
the markup, which is 1
minus c over n plus 1.

00:25:00.600 --> 00:25:05.900 align:middle line:84%
So it turns out this is 1
minus c over n plus 1 squared.

00:25:05.900 --> 00:25:09.840 align:middle line:90%


00:25:09.840 --> 00:25:13.600 align:middle line:90%
So that's the Nash equilibrium.

00:25:13.600 --> 00:25:15.200 align:middle line:84%
Now, the question
is, how could they

00:25:15.200 --> 00:25:18.960 align:middle line:90%
do better in the stage game?

00:25:18.960 --> 00:25:28.480 align:middle line:84%
So I'll ask can firms do better
than the Nash equilibrium?

00:25:28.480 --> 00:25:30.100 align:middle line:84%
This may not be a
Nash equilibrium,

00:25:30.100 --> 00:25:31.808 align:middle line:84%
but I'm just saying,
is there another way

00:25:31.808 --> 00:25:35.200 align:middle line:84%
they could produce to do better
than the Nash equilibrium?

00:25:35.200 --> 00:25:37.040 align:middle line:84%
And the idea is, so
let's say suppose

00:25:37.040 --> 00:25:39.960 align:middle line:84%
the firms wanted to make
the sum of all their profits

00:25:39.960 --> 00:25:41.028 align:middle line:90%
as high as possible.

00:25:41.028 --> 00:25:41.820 align:middle line:90%
What would they do?

00:25:41.820 --> 00:25:43.153 align:middle line:90%
So let's be a bit more specific.

00:25:43.153 --> 00:25:44.120 align:middle line:90%
How they do better?

00:25:44.120 --> 00:25:48.400 align:middle line:84%
Well, what they
could do is maximize

00:25:48.400 --> 00:25:49.860 align:middle line:90%
the sum of their profits.

00:25:49.860 --> 00:25:55.983 align:middle line:90%


00:25:55.983 --> 00:25:56.900 align:middle line:90%
How would you do this?

00:25:56.900 --> 00:25:58.740 align:middle line:84%
How would the firms maximize
the sum of their profits?

00:25:58.740 --> 00:25:59.080 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:25:59.080 --> 00:26:00.760 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: You could
choose exactly one firm

00:26:00.760 --> 00:26:01.740 align:middle line:90%
to produce everything.

00:26:01.740 --> 00:26:04.220 align:middle line:84%
And the reason is,
in that case, n is 1,

00:26:04.220 --> 00:26:06.720 align:middle line:84%
so then the market
can be higher.

00:26:06.720 --> 00:26:07.800 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: That's right.

00:26:07.800 --> 00:26:09.300 align:middle line:84%
So I think we're
getting very close.

00:26:09.300 --> 00:26:10.160 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:26:10.160 --> 00:26:12.540 align:middle line:90%
They want to think about--

00:26:12.540 --> 00:26:14.640 align:middle line:84%
so if only one firm
produced, how much--

00:26:14.640 --> 00:26:17.680 align:middle line:84%
what would be the right amount
for that firm to produce?

00:26:17.680 --> 00:26:21.360 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Then that firm would
produce 1 minus c over 2.

00:26:21.360 --> 00:26:22.066 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Right.

00:26:22.066 --> 00:26:22.420 align:middle line:90%
Exactly.

00:26:22.420 --> 00:26:24.120 align:middle line:84%
And we can actually--
there's a few different ways

00:26:24.120 --> 00:26:24.953 align:middle line:90%
we can achieve that.

00:26:24.953 --> 00:26:29.023 align:middle line:84%
So the key is, if we want to
maximize the sum of our profits,

00:26:29.023 --> 00:26:30.440 align:middle line:84%
what we want to
do-- and this is--

00:26:30.440 --> 00:26:32.357 align:middle line:84%
what you described as a
special case of this--

00:26:32.357 --> 00:26:34.720 align:middle line:84%
we want to collectively
act as a monopolist.

00:26:34.720 --> 00:26:37.285 align:middle line:84%
We want to make sure that the
sum of the amounts we produce

00:26:37.285 --> 00:26:39.160 align:middle line:84%
is exactly the amount
that a monopolist would

00:26:39.160 --> 00:26:43.200 align:middle line:84%
produce because we know that a
monopolist maximizes profits.

00:26:43.200 --> 00:26:46.280 align:middle line:84%
So to maximize the sum of
the profits, what we need

00:26:46.280 --> 00:26:52.960 align:middle line:84%
is we need q1 plus qn to equal
the quantity that maximizes

00:26:52.960 --> 00:26:53.940 align:middle line:90%
the sum of the profits.

00:26:53.940 --> 00:26:56.480 align:middle line:84%
It turns out that's exactly
the monopoly quantity.

00:26:56.480 --> 00:26:59.180 align:middle line:84%
So we want this to be
the monopoly quantity.

00:26:59.180 --> 00:27:03.840 align:middle line:90%


00:27:03.840 --> 00:27:04.380 align:middle line:90%
Why is this?

00:27:04.380 --> 00:27:07.200 align:middle line:84%
Well, the trick
is if I produce q1

00:27:07.200 --> 00:27:11.040 align:middle line:84%
and firm 2 produces q2 all the
way up to firm n, producing qn,

00:27:11.040 --> 00:27:14.320 align:middle line:84%
the sum of the firm's profits is
exactly the same as the profit

00:27:14.320 --> 00:27:17.920 align:middle line:84%
that a single firm would make if
they produced q1 all the way up

00:27:17.920 --> 00:27:20.080 align:middle line:90%
to qn by themselves.

00:27:20.080 --> 00:27:21.895 align:middle line:84%
And the way that we
maximize that amount

00:27:21.895 --> 00:27:24.520 align:middle line:84%
is exactly by setting it equal
to the monopoly quantity, which,

00:27:24.520 --> 00:27:28.000 align:middle line:84%
in this example, is
1 minus c over 2.

00:27:28.000 --> 00:27:30.000 align:middle line:84%
So what you described
is one special case

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:32.618 align:middle line:84%
of this, where one firm could
produce the monopoly quantity

00:27:32.618 --> 00:27:34.160 align:middle line:84%
and everyone else
could produce zero.

00:27:34.160 --> 00:27:36.040 align:middle line:84%
But I don't think that would
be a very effective cartel.

00:27:36.040 --> 00:27:38.160 align:middle line:84%
If I said, hey, other
countries, I want you to all

00:27:38.160 --> 00:27:40.407 align:middle line:84%
to produce zero, and I'm
going to produce everything,

00:27:40.407 --> 00:27:42.740 align:middle line:84%
I don't think those other
countries would be very happy.

00:27:42.740 --> 00:27:45.320 align:middle line:84%
So probably we would split
it a bit differently.

00:27:45.320 --> 00:27:47.040 align:middle line:84%
A natural split would
be an even split,

00:27:47.040 --> 00:27:50.440 align:middle line:84%
where each of us produces
1 minus c over 2n.

00:27:50.440 --> 00:27:56.440 align:middle line:84%
So one thing we could do--
so, e,g., we could do q1--

00:27:56.440 --> 00:27:58.080 align:middle line:90%
I'll go down here.

00:27:58.080 --> 00:28:09.440 align:middle line:84%
The even split would be we each
produce maybe what I'll call qM.

00:28:09.440 --> 00:28:13.420 align:middle line:84%
So before, I described the
Nash equilibrium quantity.

00:28:13.420 --> 00:28:14.972 align:middle line:84%
Now I'm going to
describe-- when I

00:28:14.972 --> 00:28:16.680 align:middle line:84%
say monopoly quantity,
what I really mean

00:28:16.680 --> 00:28:19.520 align:middle line:84%
is each firm's share of
the monopoly quantity.

00:28:19.520 --> 00:28:25.600 align:middle line:90%
So this is 1 minus c over 2n.

00:28:25.600 --> 00:28:29.403 align:middle line:84%
So qM is technically 1 n-th
of the monopoly quantity.

00:28:29.403 --> 00:28:31.320 align:middle line:84%
Because it's the quantity
that each firm needs

00:28:31.320 --> 00:28:33.680 align:middle line:84%
to produce to ensure
that the total production

00:28:33.680 --> 00:28:36.840 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the
monopoly quantity.

00:28:36.840 --> 00:28:51.893 align:middle line:84%
Now, if we compare these two
numbers, what do you see?

00:28:51.893 --> 00:28:54.060 align:middle line:84%
Which is bigger, and what's
your intuition for that?

00:28:54.060 --> 00:28:56.600 align:middle line:90%


00:28:56.600 --> 00:29:01.560 align:middle line:84%
Let's assume n is at
least 1-- at least 2.

00:29:01.560 --> 00:29:04.480 align:middle line:90%
So which one is bigger and why?

00:29:04.480 --> 00:29:05.580 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:29:05.580 --> 00:29:07.560 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: The Nash
equilibrium is larger.

00:29:07.560 --> 00:29:09.040 align:middle line:84%
And the intuition
for that could be

00:29:09.040 --> 00:29:11.960 align:middle line:84%
that in order to do better
than Nash equilibrium,

00:29:11.960 --> 00:29:16.800 align:middle line:84%
the firms kind of need to have a
closer collaboration to maximize

00:29:16.800 --> 00:29:19.160 align:middle line:90%
as if they were a single firm.

00:29:19.160 --> 00:29:22.320 align:middle line:84%
Whereas, each of them in
that non-Nash equilibrium

00:29:22.320 --> 00:29:23.880 align:middle line:84%
state, a monopolist
state, would be

00:29:23.880 --> 00:29:26.940 align:middle line:84%
kind of incentivized to
produce more to capture profit.

00:29:26.940 --> 00:29:27.940 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly right.

00:29:27.940 --> 00:29:31.560 align:middle line:84%
So this is exactly what we
talked about at the beginning.

00:29:31.560 --> 00:29:33.440 align:middle line:84%
In the Nash
equilibrium, the firms

00:29:33.440 --> 00:29:36.140 align:middle line:84%
are behaving optimally given the
way everyone else is behaving.

00:29:36.140 --> 00:29:38.240 align:middle line:84%
But that actually turns
out that collectively it

00:29:38.240 --> 00:29:40.320 align:middle line:84%
hurts them because they're
producing too much.

00:29:40.320 --> 00:29:43.600 align:middle line:84%
They would actually do better
if they each produced less.

00:29:43.600 --> 00:29:46.480 align:middle line:84%
If they each produce less,
then the market price

00:29:46.480 --> 00:29:51.873 align:middle line:84%
is going to be higher, and
the payoff of each firm

00:29:51.873 --> 00:29:53.540 align:middle line:84%
is actually going to
be strictly higher.

00:29:53.540 --> 00:29:55.800 align:middle line:84%
The problem is this will
not be in equilibrium.

00:29:55.800 --> 00:29:58.380 align:middle line:84%
And what will happen
is suppose we did this.

00:29:58.380 --> 00:29:59.360 align:middle line:90%
Let's just check.

00:29:59.360 --> 00:30:01.720 align:middle line:84%
This is not going to
be a Nash equilibrium.

00:30:01.720 --> 00:30:02.360 align:middle line:90%
And why?

00:30:02.360 --> 00:30:05.420 align:middle line:84%
What is the deviation
for, say, firm i?

00:30:05.420 --> 00:30:07.745 align:middle line:84%
What would they-- how
could they do better?

00:30:07.745 --> 00:30:10.120 align:middle line:84%
Would they want to increase
or decrease their production?

00:30:10.120 --> 00:30:11.495 align:middle line:84%
Increase their
production, right?

00:30:11.495 --> 00:30:16.120 align:middle line:84%
So it's not a Nash
equilibrium because firm i

00:30:16.120 --> 00:30:27.980 align:middle line:84%
prefers to strictly
increase production.

00:30:27.980 --> 00:30:31.158 align:middle line:90%


00:30:31.158 --> 00:30:32.700 align:middle line:84%
And we can check
that mathematically.

00:30:32.700 --> 00:30:35.320 align:middle line:84%
But let's just-- this seems
a little puzzling now,

00:30:35.320 --> 00:30:37.680 align:middle line:84%
and this is getting at
a core game theory idea.

00:30:37.680 --> 00:30:39.760 align:middle line:84%
We said that these
production levels maximize

00:30:39.760 --> 00:30:41.680 align:middle line:90%
the sum of everyone's profits.

00:30:41.680 --> 00:30:44.920 align:middle line:84%
But now I'm saying one firm can
do better by strictly increasing

00:30:44.920 --> 00:30:46.200 align:middle line:90%
how much they produce.

00:30:46.200 --> 00:30:47.307 align:middle line:90%
So how is that consistent?

00:30:47.307 --> 00:30:49.140 align:middle line:84%
I thought we were already
maximizing profit.

00:30:49.140 --> 00:30:52.240 align:middle line:84%
So how can one firm do
better by deviating?

00:30:52.240 --> 00:30:53.280 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, on the front?

00:30:53.280 --> 00:30:55.590 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: You're maximizing
the [? sum ?] [INAUDIBLE].

00:30:55.590 --> 00:30:56.340 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly.

00:30:56.340 --> 00:30:57.173 align:middle line:90%
So what must happen?

00:30:57.173 --> 00:31:00.900 align:middle line:84%
If this firm produces strictly
more, this firm is doing better.

00:31:00.900 --> 00:31:04.208 align:middle line:84%
But what do we know
must also happen?

00:31:04.208 --> 00:31:05.500 align:middle line:90%
The other ones are doing worse.

00:31:05.500 --> 00:31:07.000 align:middle line:84%
In fact, the amount by
which they're doing worse

00:31:07.000 --> 00:31:09.500 align:middle line:84%
more than offsets the amount
that this firm is doing better.

00:31:09.500 --> 00:31:10.940 align:middle line:90%
So I benefit myself.

00:31:10.940 --> 00:31:12.240 align:middle line:90%
I hurt everyone else.

00:31:12.240 --> 00:31:14.520 align:middle line:84%
On average, the sum
of payoffs goes down

00:31:14.520 --> 00:31:16.980 align:middle line:84%
because I hurt everyone else
more than I help myself.

00:31:16.980 --> 00:31:19.080 align:middle line:84%
But I don't care because
I'm helping myself.

00:31:19.080 --> 00:31:21.180 align:middle line:84%
That's the basic
intuition of this.

00:31:21.180 --> 00:31:23.820 align:middle line:90%


00:31:23.820 --> 00:31:29.200 align:middle line:84%
So now the question is, can
we sustain the monopoly levels

00:31:29.200 --> 00:31:30.420 align:middle line:90%
in equilibrium?

00:31:30.420 --> 00:31:33.460 align:middle line:90%
So the question is--

00:31:33.460 --> 00:31:36.880 align:middle line:90%


00:31:36.880 --> 00:31:37.940 align:middle line:90%
here's a question.

00:31:37.940 --> 00:31:50.397 align:middle line:90%


00:31:50.397 --> 00:31:52.480 align:middle line:84%
So we're all better off
if every period we produce

00:31:52.480 --> 00:31:54.480 align:middle line:90%
qM rather than qNE.

00:31:54.480 --> 00:31:56.400 align:middle line:90%
That would be great for us.

00:31:56.400 --> 00:31:58.200 align:middle line:84%
It's not clear, though,
how we can sustain

00:31:58.200 --> 00:32:00.360 align:middle line:84%
this in a self-enforcing
way because we already

00:32:00.360 --> 00:32:02.280 align:middle line:84%
said if we try to
do this, one firm

00:32:02.280 --> 00:32:04.640 align:middle line:84%
can strictly benefit by
deviating and producing

00:32:04.640 --> 00:32:06.920 align:middle line:90%
slightly more.

00:32:06.920 --> 00:32:07.900 align:middle line:90%
So how can we do this?

00:32:07.900 --> 00:32:10.240 align:middle line:90%
Well, what's the idea?

00:32:10.240 --> 00:32:13.140 align:middle line:84%
We need some way to punish
people for overproduction.

00:32:13.140 --> 00:32:14.640 align:middle line:84%
And, in fact, we'll
just punish them

00:32:14.640 --> 00:32:17.182 align:middle line:84%
if they produce anything other
than what they're supposed to.

00:32:17.182 --> 00:32:19.620 align:middle line:84%
Really, overproduction is
the tempting thing to do.

00:32:19.620 --> 00:32:20.740 align:middle line:90%
But here's the idea.

00:32:20.740 --> 00:32:28.020 align:middle line:84%
We want to punish firms
for overproduction.

00:32:28.020 --> 00:32:39.080 align:middle line:90%


00:32:39.080 --> 00:32:43.840 align:middle line:84%
So any ideas about how we
might be able to do this?

00:32:43.840 --> 00:32:45.340 align:middle line:90%
So we're getting together.

00:32:45.340 --> 00:32:46.820 align:middle line:84%
We see that one
firm has deviated.

00:32:46.820 --> 00:32:48.280 align:middle line:90%
Say, they've produced too much.

00:32:48.280 --> 00:32:50.740 align:middle line:84%
Now, what might we want to
do to try to punish them?

00:32:50.740 --> 00:32:54.280 align:middle line:90%


00:32:54.280 --> 00:32:54.920 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:32:54.920 --> 00:32:57.730 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: We could all move to
the Nash equilibrium quantities.

00:32:57.730 --> 00:32:58.480 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly.

00:32:58.480 --> 00:33:00.147 align:middle line:90%
So I think the first--

00:33:00.147 --> 00:33:01.480 align:middle line:90%
that's exactly where were going.

00:33:01.480 --> 00:33:02.660 align:middle line:84%
I was expecting a
different answer.

00:33:02.660 --> 00:33:03.100 align:middle line:90%
So well done.

00:33:03.100 --> 00:33:03.860 align:middle line:90%
You jumped ahead.

00:33:03.860 --> 00:33:04.800 align:middle line:84%
What I would expect
people to say

00:33:04.800 --> 00:33:06.327 align:middle line:84%
is we would just
flood the market.

00:33:06.327 --> 00:33:08.660 align:middle line:84%
The problem with that is that
may not be self-enforcing,

00:33:08.660 --> 00:33:09.800 align:middle line:84%
because why does
the firm actually

00:33:09.800 --> 00:33:10.842 align:middle line:90%
want to flood the market?

00:33:10.842 --> 00:33:12.500 align:middle line:84%
So indeed, what
you're suggesting--

00:33:12.500 --> 00:33:13.900 align:middle line:84%
I mean, we could think
about doing that later.

00:33:13.900 --> 00:33:16.525 align:middle line:84%
But indeed, the natural thing to
do is, the way we could punish

00:33:16.525 --> 00:33:18.460 align:middle line:84%
is we could revert to
the Nash equilibrium.

00:33:18.460 --> 00:33:21.300 align:middle line:84%
Remember, last class when we
talked about the folk theorem,

00:33:21.300 --> 00:33:24.380 align:middle line:84%
we described these strategies
where if anyone deviated,

00:33:24.380 --> 00:33:25.880 align:middle line:84%
we reverted to the
Nash equilibrium.

00:33:25.880 --> 00:33:34.920 align:middle line:84%
In this case-- so
by reverting to qNE.

00:33:34.920 --> 00:33:36.800 align:middle line:84%
So what this says is
we're going to try

00:33:36.800 --> 00:33:38.140 align:middle line:90%
to produce the monopoly level.

00:33:38.140 --> 00:33:39.540 align:middle line:84%
We're each going to
produce a small amount.

00:33:39.540 --> 00:33:42.165 align:middle line:84%
We're going to get high profits
because the price is very high.

00:33:42.165 --> 00:33:44.560 align:middle line:84%
If we observe anyone
deviating, well, now we're

00:33:44.560 --> 00:33:46.740 align:middle line:84%
all going to produce the
Nash equilibrium level.

00:33:46.740 --> 00:33:48.500 align:middle line:84%
We're all worse off,
but we're actually

00:33:48.500 --> 00:33:50.000 align:middle line:84%
willing to carry
out this punishment

00:33:50.000 --> 00:33:51.740 align:middle line:84%
because this is itself
a Nash equilibrium.

00:33:51.740 --> 00:33:52.407 align:middle line:90%
That's the idea.

00:33:52.407 --> 00:33:55.480 align:middle line:84%
And now we want to
formally check this.

00:33:55.480 --> 00:33:57.720 align:middle line:84%
I guess first question,
will this always work?

00:33:57.720 --> 00:33:59.137 align:middle line:84%
What are we going
to need for this

00:33:59.137 --> 00:34:01.120 align:middle line:84%
to work, for this to be
a subgame perfect Nash

00:34:01.120 --> 00:34:01.860 align:middle line:90%
equilibrium?

00:34:01.860 --> 00:34:06.027 align:middle line:90%


00:34:06.027 --> 00:34:07.860 align:middle line:84%
What's going to need
to be true about delta?

00:34:07.860 --> 00:34:12.760 align:middle line:90%


00:34:12.760 --> 00:34:13.340 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:34:13.340 --> 00:34:15.382 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: It needs to be
sufficiently large enough

00:34:15.382 --> 00:34:17.739 align:middle line:84%
that we kind of value the
profits in future periods.

00:34:17.739 --> 00:34:18.739 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Yeah, exactly.

00:34:18.739 --> 00:34:20.300 align:middle line:84%
So our guess-- I mean,
we'll formally check this,

00:34:20.300 --> 00:34:22.300 align:middle line:84%
but it's always good to
make a guess in advance.

00:34:22.300 --> 00:34:29.000 align:middle line:84%
Our guess is that this will
work if delta is high enough.

00:34:29.000 --> 00:34:32.180 align:middle line:90%


00:34:32.180 --> 00:34:35.840 align:middle line:84%
Because the idea is I can
benefit today by overproducing.

00:34:35.840 --> 00:34:37.960 align:middle line:84%
The only reason I'm not
going to do that is I'm

00:34:37.960 --> 00:34:39.780 align:middle line:84%
afraid of this
punishment in the future.

00:34:39.780 --> 00:34:42.460 align:middle line:84%
But I have to be patient enough
that I care about that future.

00:34:42.460 --> 00:34:44.675 align:middle line:84%
If I only care about profits
today, then, of course,

00:34:44.675 --> 00:34:46.300 align:middle line:84%
it's not going to be
a Nash equilibrium

00:34:46.300 --> 00:34:49.620 align:middle line:84%
because all I care about is
the benefit that I get today.

00:34:49.620 --> 00:34:52.500 align:middle line:84%
So now we're going to actually
construct this equilibrium.

00:34:52.500 --> 00:34:59.160 align:middle line:90%
So let's start here.

00:34:59.160 --> 00:34:59.660 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:34:59.660 --> 00:35:19.080 align:middle line:90%


00:35:19.080 --> 00:35:28.240 align:middle line:84%
So now let's construct the
SPNE that has this form.

00:35:28.240 --> 00:35:33.217 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be helpful
to define two functions,

00:35:33.217 --> 00:35:34.800 align:middle line:84%
just because if we
don't define these,

00:35:34.800 --> 00:35:36.300 align:middle line:84%
the algebra gets
really complicated.

00:35:36.300 --> 00:35:42.960 align:middle line:84%
So let's just make two
definitions-- f of q and g of q.

00:35:42.960 --> 00:35:45.400 align:middle line:84%
So for any quantity
level q, f of q

00:35:45.400 --> 00:35:49.880 align:middle line:84%
is going to say if we all
produce q, what is our path?

00:35:49.880 --> 00:35:57.400 align:middle line:84%
So mathematically this is
q times 1 minus nq minus c.

00:35:57.400 --> 00:36:00.872 align:middle line:84%
And technically, this only makes
sense if q is less than 1 over

00:36:00.872 --> 00:36:03.580 align:middle line:84%
n, so that this number doesn't--
so prices don't become negative.

00:36:03.580 --> 00:36:05.480 align:middle line:84%
So this says if
we all produce q,

00:36:05.480 --> 00:36:07.720 align:middle line:84%
the market price is
going to be 1 minus nq

00:36:07.720 --> 00:36:10.120 align:middle line:84%
because nq is the
market quantity.

00:36:10.120 --> 00:36:12.480 align:middle line:84%
So 1 minus nq is
the market price.

00:36:12.480 --> 00:36:15.460 align:middle line:84%
And if we subtract c from that,
we get the profit per unit,

00:36:15.460 --> 00:36:18.520 align:middle line:90%
and then we multiply it by q.

00:36:18.520 --> 00:36:20.840 align:middle line:84%
And then the other thing
we want to understand

00:36:20.840 --> 00:36:26.600 align:middle line:84%
is how well can a firm do if
everyone else is playing q,

00:36:26.600 --> 00:36:30.338 align:middle line:84%
and I choose my best
deviation in the stage game?

00:36:30.338 --> 00:36:31.880 align:middle line:84%
This is going to be
the key deviation

00:36:31.880 --> 00:36:32.620 align:middle line:90%
we have to worry about.

00:36:32.620 --> 00:36:33.980 align:middle line:84%
So we want to have
notation for this.

00:36:33.980 --> 00:36:35.522 align:middle line:84%
So this is going to
say, what happens

00:36:35.522 --> 00:36:39.880 align:middle line:84%
if I choose my quantity
level q prime to maximize

00:36:39.880 --> 00:36:44.160 align:middle line:84%
my profit given that all the
other firms are choosing q?

00:36:44.160 --> 00:36:47.560 align:middle line:84%
Well, if I choose q prime,
this is how much I produce.

00:36:47.560 --> 00:36:49.800 align:middle line:90%
What is the price going to be?

00:36:49.800 --> 00:36:54.940 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's going to be 1 minus
n minus 1q minus q prime,

00:36:54.940 --> 00:36:57.160 align:middle line:90%
and then I subtract c.

00:36:57.160 --> 00:37:00.960 align:middle line:84%
So here, the price is 1
minus the total quantity

00:37:00.960 --> 00:37:02.080 align:middle line:90%
on the market.

00:37:02.080 --> 00:37:03.735 align:middle line:84%
The total quantity
of the market is

00:37:03.735 --> 00:37:05.360 align:middle line:84%
what's produced by
everyone else, which

00:37:05.360 --> 00:37:07.160 align:middle line:90%
is q times n minus 1.

00:37:07.160 --> 00:37:09.160 align:middle line:84%
Because there's n
minus 1 other firms.

00:37:09.160 --> 00:37:11.160 align:middle line:90%
They're each producing q.

00:37:11.160 --> 00:37:15.640 align:middle line:84%
If I choose q prime,
then this gives me

00:37:15.640 --> 00:37:17.100 align:middle line:90%
the total amount produced.

00:37:17.100 --> 00:37:18.580 align:middle line:90%
This gives me the price.

00:37:18.580 --> 00:37:22.040 align:middle line:90%
And then I subtract my cost.

00:37:22.040 --> 00:37:28.743 align:middle line:84%
And if we could actually
do a little algebra.

00:37:28.743 --> 00:37:30.160 align:middle line:84%
I don't expect
people to see this,

00:37:30.160 --> 00:37:36.483 align:middle line:84%
but I think it's actually
going to be this.

00:37:36.483 --> 00:37:37.900 align:middle line:84%
The formula doesn't
really matter,

00:37:37.900 --> 00:37:39.067 align:middle line:90%
but this is what it will be.

00:37:39.067 --> 00:37:43.200 align:middle line:90%


00:37:43.200 --> 00:37:46.380 align:middle line:84%
But we'll just stick with
these two formulas here.

00:37:46.380 --> 00:37:50.420 align:middle line:90%


00:37:50.420 --> 00:37:54.640 align:middle line:84%
And then what is our
strategy profile?

00:37:54.640 --> 00:37:56.100 align:middle line:90%
Well, it's exactly what we said.

00:37:56.100 --> 00:38:00.360 align:middle line:90%


00:38:00.360 --> 00:38:12.880 align:middle line:90%
So one, each firm produces qM.

00:38:12.880 --> 00:38:14.060 align:middle line:90%
Until a deviation.

00:38:14.060 --> 00:38:19.440 align:middle line:90%


00:38:19.440 --> 00:38:21.980 align:middle line:90%
But two, if anyone has deviated.

00:38:21.980 --> 00:38:31.920 align:middle line:90%


00:38:31.920 --> 00:38:39.058 align:middle line:84%
every firm produces qNE So this
is just formally writing down

00:38:39.058 --> 00:38:40.600 align:middle line:84%
the Nash reversion
idea that we said.

00:38:40.600 --> 00:38:43.480 align:middle line:84%
We start by producing
qM every period.

00:38:43.480 --> 00:38:45.660 align:middle line:84%
As long as no one's
deviated, that's what we do.

00:38:45.660 --> 00:38:48.000 align:middle line:84%
But if any firm
deviates, then we all

00:38:48.000 --> 00:38:50.720 align:middle line:84%
revert to the Nash
equilibrium quantities, qNE.

00:38:50.720 --> 00:38:54.520 align:middle line:90%


00:38:54.520 --> 00:38:58.480 align:middle line:84%
So now we want to check that
this is a subgame perfect Nash

00:38:58.480 --> 00:39:00.680 align:middle line:90%
equilibrium.

00:39:00.680 --> 00:39:03.320 align:middle line:84%
As usual, we're going to use the
one-shot deviation principle.

00:39:03.320 --> 00:39:04.903 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to
check every history

00:39:04.903 --> 00:39:08.278 align:middle line:84%
and check that no firm has a
profitable one-shot deviation.

00:39:08.278 --> 00:39:10.320 align:middle line:84%
But we're going to use a
different representation

00:39:10.320 --> 00:39:12.720 align:middle line:84%
of this strategy called
an automaton, which

00:39:12.720 --> 00:39:15.353 align:middle line:84%
is going to turn out to be
very useful for structuring

00:39:15.353 --> 00:39:17.020 align:middle line:84%
the way we think about
these equilibria.

00:39:17.020 --> 00:39:22.080 align:middle line:84%
So let's look at what's called
the automaton representation--

00:39:22.080 --> 00:39:27.360 align:middle line:90%
automaton representation.

00:39:27.360 --> 00:39:29.700 align:middle line:84%
So you recall that when we
describe these strategies,

00:39:29.700 --> 00:39:32.640 align:middle line:84%
the way we analyze them is we
often grouped all the histories

00:39:32.640 --> 00:39:33.468 align:middle line:90%
into two buckets.

00:39:33.468 --> 00:39:35.760 align:middle line:84%
We said they're the histories
where someone's deviated,

00:39:35.760 --> 00:39:38.120 align:middle line:84%
and the histories where
no one's deviated.

00:39:38.120 --> 00:39:40.760 align:middle line:84%
And the automaton
representation formalizes

00:39:40.760 --> 00:39:43.080 align:middle line:90%
that in terms of states.

00:39:43.080 --> 00:39:46.082 align:middle line:84%
We think of, what's the
current state that we're in,

00:39:46.082 --> 00:39:47.540 align:middle line:84%
and how do we behave
in that state?

00:39:47.540 --> 00:39:48.748 align:middle line:90%
So let me just write it down.

00:39:48.748 --> 00:39:51.960 align:middle line:84%
To represent this equilibrium,
we're going to need two states.

00:39:51.960 --> 00:39:56.880 align:middle line:84%
We'll call it the monopoly
state and the stage game Nash

00:39:56.880 --> 00:39:59.480 align:middle line:90%
equilibrium state.

00:39:59.480 --> 00:40:02.980 align:middle line:84%
And now we need to specify
how we behave in each state.

00:40:02.980 --> 00:40:04.480 align:middle line:84%
Well, in the monopoly
state, we each

00:40:04.480 --> 00:40:05.840 align:middle line:90%
produce the monopoly price--

00:40:05.840 --> 00:40:06.700 align:middle line:90%
monopoly quantity.

00:40:06.700 --> 00:40:07.200 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

00:40:07.200 --> 00:40:07.940 align:middle line:90%
That's easy.

00:40:07.940 --> 00:40:12.280 align:middle line:90%
So qi equals qM for all i.

00:40:12.280 --> 00:40:14.400 align:middle line:84%
And in the Nash
equilibrium state,

00:40:14.400 --> 00:40:19.020 align:middle line:84%
we each produce the Nash
equilibrium quantity.

00:40:19.020 --> 00:40:21.080 align:middle line:90%
That's pretty easy.

00:40:21.080 --> 00:40:23.040 align:middle line:84%
So what do I really
mean by these states?

00:40:23.040 --> 00:40:27.120 align:middle line:84%
Well, they're this
mental construct.

00:40:27.120 --> 00:40:28.940 align:middle line:84%
But let's just describe
how things work,

00:40:28.940 --> 00:40:30.500 align:middle line:84%
and then we can
maybe interpret it.

00:40:30.500 --> 00:40:32.680 align:middle line:84%
So the idea is we
start in this state.

00:40:32.680 --> 00:40:34.500 align:middle line:84%
Initially, we're each
going to produce qM.

00:40:34.500 --> 00:40:35.875 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to
put an arrow here

00:40:35.875 --> 00:40:38.600 align:middle line:84%
to say this is the
state that we start in.

00:40:38.600 --> 00:40:40.640 align:middle line:84%
And in this state, what
we're supposed to do

00:40:40.640 --> 00:40:43.400 align:middle line:84%
is we're each supposed to
produce the monopoly quantity.

00:40:43.400 --> 00:40:49.120 align:middle line:84%
Now what we have
to describe is how

00:40:49.120 --> 00:40:51.520 align:middle line:90%
we transition between states.

00:40:51.520 --> 00:40:54.065 align:middle line:84%
So the idea of
this equilibrium is

00:40:54.065 --> 00:40:55.440 align:middle line:84%
we're going to
stay in this state

00:40:55.440 --> 00:40:57.680 align:middle line:90%
as long as no one has deviated.

00:40:57.680 --> 00:41:00.720 align:middle line:84%
But if someone deviates, then
we move to a different state.

00:41:00.720 --> 00:41:05.600 align:middle line:84%
So what I'll write is I'll
write an arrow here to say,

00:41:05.600 --> 00:41:06.840 align:middle line:90%
we're in state m.

00:41:06.840 --> 00:41:09.200 align:middle line:90%
And we stay in state m if what?

00:41:09.200 --> 00:41:15.620 align:middle line:90%
If qi equals qM for all i.

00:41:15.620 --> 00:41:18.920 align:middle line:90%


00:41:18.920 --> 00:41:22.360 align:middle line:84%
That's the only way we're
going to stay in this state.

00:41:22.360 --> 00:41:25.360 align:middle line:84%
Otherwise, someone has
deviated, and then we

00:41:25.360 --> 00:41:28.480 align:middle line:90%
move to the other state.

00:41:28.480 --> 00:41:29.900 align:middle line:90%
So I'll write otherwise here.

00:41:29.900 --> 00:41:37.310 align:middle line:90%


00:41:37.310 --> 00:41:41.138 align:middle line:84%
If we're in this state,
well, once someone's deviated

00:41:41.138 --> 00:41:43.430 align:middle line:84%
and we're in this state, we
actually stay in this state

00:41:43.430 --> 00:41:43.970 align:middle line:90%
forever.

00:41:43.970 --> 00:41:45.470 align:middle line:84%
There's no way to
leave this state.

00:41:45.470 --> 00:41:49.810 align:middle line:84%
So we're just going to put an
arrow here that says, anything.

00:41:49.810 --> 00:41:53.150 align:middle line:90%


00:41:53.150 --> 00:41:57.750 align:middle line:84%
So this simple graph actually
fully describes a strategy.

00:41:57.750 --> 00:42:00.030 align:middle line:84%
And, in fact, fully describes
the strategy profile

00:42:00.030 --> 00:42:01.170 align:middle line:90%
in this equilibrium.

00:42:01.170 --> 00:42:03.030 align:middle line:84%
And I think the way I
like to visualize it--

00:42:03.030 --> 00:42:05.450 align:middle line:84%
I mean, of course, if firms
are actually colluding today,

00:42:05.450 --> 00:42:06.170 align:middle line:90%
they'd use computers.

00:42:06.170 --> 00:42:07.337 align:middle line:90%
They'd keep track of things.

00:42:07.337 --> 00:42:10.230 align:middle line:84%
But a very simple way to think
about this is all I need to do

00:42:10.230 --> 00:42:12.270 align:middle line:84%
is have two positions
on my desk--

00:42:12.270 --> 00:42:14.620 align:middle line:84%
the monopoly position and the
Nash equilibrium position.

00:42:14.620 --> 00:42:16.370 align:middle line:84%
And I like to imagine
I just have a stone.

00:42:16.370 --> 00:42:19.070 align:middle line:84%
And I start by putting the
stone in the monopoly position.

00:42:19.070 --> 00:42:20.050 align:middle line:90%
I look at my desk.

00:42:20.050 --> 00:42:22.330 align:middle line:84%
I see-- and every firm
is doing the same thing.

00:42:22.330 --> 00:42:24.190 align:middle line:84%
We start with the
monopoly position.

00:42:24.190 --> 00:42:25.810 align:middle line:90%
We all say, what do we do here?

00:42:25.810 --> 00:42:27.510 align:middle line:84%
We all produce the
monopoly level.

00:42:27.510 --> 00:42:30.590 align:middle line:84%
Then tomorrow we observe what
everyone produced yesterday.

00:42:30.590 --> 00:42:32.550 align:middle line:84%
And then we potentially
move the stone

00:42:32.550 --> 00:42:35.770 align:middle line:84%
from the monopoly position to
the Nash equilibrium position

00:42:35.770 --> 00:42:37.670 align:middle line:84%
if we observe that
someone has deviated.

00:42:37.670 --> 00:42:39.877 align:middle line:84%
If no one deviates, we
keep the stone there.

00:42:39.877 --> 00:42:41.710 align:middle line:84%
And then tomorrow we
see where the stone is,

00:42:41.710 --> 00:42:43.030 align:middle line:90%
and we decide how to play.

00:42:43.030 --> 00:42:45.510 align:middle line:84%
So this is a very
simple representation

00:42:45.510 --> 00:42:48.190 align:middle line:90%
of the strategy profile.

00:42:48.190 --> 00:42:53.310 align:middle line:84%
So now we come over
here, and let's

00:42:53.310 --> 00:42:56.490 align:middle line:84%
write down the conditions
for equilibrium in this game.

00:42:56.490 --> 00:43:07.710 align:middle line:90%


00:43:07.710 --> 00:43:11.350 align:middle line:84%
So it turns out the key
to analyzing equilibrium

00:43:11.350 --> 00:43:14.630 align:middle line:84%
using this automaton
representation is

00:43:14.630 --> 00:43:18.890 align:middle line:84%
to write down what we'll
call the value function.

00:43:18.890 --> 00:43:23.430 align:middle line:90%


00:43:23.430 --> 00:43:25.990 align:middle line:84%
And in this case, we need to
specify the value function each

00:43:25.990 --> 00:43:26.850 align:middle line:90%
of the two states.

00:43:26.850 --> 00:43:32.470 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to
have vM and vNE.

00:43:32.470 --> 00:43:36.130 align:middle line:84%
And what the value function
says is, in the first case,

00:43:36.130 --> 00:43:39.430 align:middle line:84%
suppose we're in state
M today, and everyone

00:43:39.430 --> 00:43:41.390 align:middle line:90%
follows this strategy.

00:43:41.390 --> 00:43:44.910 align:middle line:84%
What is the average discounted
payoff of the firms going to be?

00:43:44.910 --> 00:43:47.870 align:middle line:84%
And then vNE says, well,
if we're in state NE,

00:43:47.870 --> 00:43:49.630 align:middle line:84%
and we all follow
that strategy, what

00:43:49.630 --> 00:43:52.950 align:middle line:84%
is the average discounted
payoff of each firm going to be?

00:43:52.950 --> 00:43:57.205 align:middle line:84%
So let's start with M.
If we're in state M,

00:43:57.205 --> 00:43:59.830 align:middle line:84%
It's a symmetric equilibrium, so
we're just going to write down

00:43:59.830 --> 00:44:00.810 align:middle line:90%
one firm's payoffs.

00:44:00.810 --> 00:44:02.685 align:middle line:84%
All the other firms are
going to be the same.

00:44:02.685 --> 00:44:04.910 align:middle line:84%
If we're in state M, and
we all follow the strategy,

00:44:04.910 --> 00:44:09.930 align:middle line:84%
what is the expected discounted
average payoff of each firm?

00:44:09.930 --> 00:44:12.412 align:middle line:90%


00:44:12.412 --> 00:44:13.870 align:middle line:84%
Well, first let's
see what happens.

00:44:13.870 --> 00:44:19.592 align:middle line:84%
If we're in M, well today we all
produced a monopoly quantity.

00:44:19.592 --> 00:44:22.050 align:middle line:84%
We say in M. Tomorrow we all
produce the monopoly quantity.

00:44:22.050 --> 00:44:23.090 align:middle line:84%
We stay an M. The
next day, we all

00:44:23.090 --> 00:44:24.290 align:middle line:90%
produce the monopoly quantity.

00:44:24.290 --> 00:44:25.707 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to
get the monopoly

00:44:25.707 --> 00:44:27.150 align:middle line:90%
quantity every single period.

00:44:27.150 --> 00:44:29.010 align:middle line:84%
And what is the
payoff from that--

00:44:29.010 --> 00:44:31.590 align:middle line:90%


00:44:31.590 --> 00:44:35.150 align:middle line:84%
the average discounted
payoff from that?

00:44:35.150 --> 00:44:40.830 align:middle line:90%
Well, it's just f of qM.

00:44:40.830 --> 00:44:44.350 align:middle line:84%
f Of q is a function that tells
me what is my payoff if we all

00:44:44.350 --> 00:44:46.510 align:middle line:90%
produce quantity q?

00:44:46.510 --> 00:44:49.610 align:middle line:84%
So if we all produce quantity
qM, this is exactly my payoff.

00:44:49.610 --> 00:44:55.350 align:middle line:90%


00:44:55.350 --> 00:44:58.790 align:middle line:90%
What about vNE?

00:44:58.790 --> 00:45:02.390 align:middle line:90%


00:45:02.390 --> 00:45:06.450 align:middle line:84%
Well, here it's
going to be f qNE.

00:45:06.450 --> 00:45:09.050 align:middle line:90%


00:45:09.050 --> 00:45:11.630 align:middle line:84%
This is what are my average
discounted payoff from here

00:45:11.630 --> 00:45:14.950 align:middle line:84%
on if we're in
state in this state.

00:45:14.950 --> 00:45:17.670 align:middle line:84%
So now we're going to use the
value functions to write down

00:45:17.670 --> 00:45:19.090 align:middle line:90%
the equilibrium conditions.

00:45:19.090 --> 00:45:28.790 align:middle line:90%


00:45:28.790 --> 00:45:31.730 align:middle line:84%
And when we use an
automaton representation,

00:45:31.730 --> 00:45:34.750 align:middle line:84%
we have one equilibrium
condition for every state.

00:45:34.750 --> 00:45:40.430 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to have state
M, and we have state NE.

00:45:40.430 --> 00:45:42.150 align:middle line:84%
And what our equilibrium
condition says

00:45:42.150 --> 00:45:45.770 align:middle line:84%
is if we're in state in this--
let's take this condition.

00:45:45.770 --> 00:45:47.830 align:middle line:84%
If we're in state
M, then each firm

00:45:47.830 --> 00:45:51.210 align:middle line:84%
is better off continuing with
their equilibrium strategy

00:45:51.210 --> 00:45:54.150 align:middle line:84%
than choosing the best
possible one-shot deviation.

00:45:54.150 --> 00:45:58.290 align:middle line:84%
So each equilibrium is going
to have the following form.

00:45:58.290 --> 00:46:01.590 align:middle line:90%


00:46:01.590 --> 00:46:03.510 align:middle line:84%
In state M, we need
to check that if I

00:46:03.510 --> 00:46:06.430 align:middle line:84%
do what I'm supposed
to, well, by definition

00:46:06.430 --> 00:46:07.297 align:middle line:90%
I get my payoff vM.

00:46:07.297 --> 00:46:09.630 align:middle line:84%
That's what it means if we
continue with the equilibrium

00:46:09.630 --> 00:46:12.230 align:middle line:84%
strategy in state M.
And we have to compare

00:46:12.230 --> 00:46:14.190 align:middle line:84%
that to the best
possible payoff I could

00:46:14.190 --> 00:46:16.670 align:middle line:90%
get from a one-shot deviation.

00:46:16.670 --> 00:46:18.230 align:middle line:84%
And then we'll do
the same in NE.

00:46:18.230 --> 00:46:19.450 align:middle line:90%
Here we are today.

00:46:19.450 --> 00:46:22.170 align:middle line:84%
This is what I'll get if I
follow the equilibrium strategy.

00:46:22.170 --> 00:46:23.190 align:middle line:84%
And on the right-hand
side we want

00:46:23.190 --> 00:46:25.310 align:middle line:84%
to say, what is the best
possible payoff I could

00:46:25.310 --> 00:46:28.750 align:middle line:90%
get from a one-shot deviation?

00:46:28.750 --> 00:46:30.950 align:middle line:90%
So let's start--

00:46:30.950 --> 00:46:33.770 align:middle line:84%
I think maybe this
one is easier.

00:46:33.770 --> 00:46:36.270 align:middle line:90%


00:46:36.270 --> 00:46:36.770 align:middle line:90%
Oh, no.

00:46:36.770 --> 00:46:38.062 align:middle line:90%
We can start with M. It's fine.

00:46:38.062 --> 00:46:41.030 align:middle line:90%
So what is the best possible--

00:46:41.030 --> 00:46:42.670 align:middle line:84%
if I'm going to
deviate, how should I

00:46:42.670 --> 00:46:45.310 align:middle line:84%
deviate when we're
in state M. Yeah?

00:46:45.310 --> 00:46:47.170 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE].

00:46:47.170 --> 00:46:48.170 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly right.

00:46:48.170 --> 00:46:49.290 align:middle line:84%
So if I'm going
to deviate at all,

00:46:49.290 --> 00:46:50.490 align:middle line:90%
I know I'm going to be punished.

00:46:50.490 --> 00:46:52.490 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to be punished
in exactly the same way

00:46:52.490 --> 00:46:53.702 align:middle line:90%
no matter how I deviate.

00:46:53.702 --> 00:46:55.910 align:middle line:84%
So I might as well choose
the deviation that gives me

00:46:55.910 --> 00:46:57.090 align:middle line:90%
the highest payoff today.

00:46:57.090 --> 00:46:58.910 align:middle line:84%
And that's exactly--
and my payoff

00:46:58.910 --> 00:47:02.270 align:middle line:84%
from that is exactly
given by this function g.

00:47:02.270 --> 00:47:04.830 align:middle line:84%
So the best possible
deviation-- well,

00:47:04.830 --> 00:47:09.710 align:middle line:90%
today I'm going to get g of qM.

00:47:09.710 --> 00:47:11.670 align:middle line:84%
Remember, this doesn't
mean I'm playing qM.

00:47:11.670 --> 00:47:13.910 align:middle line:84%
This means if everyone
else is playing qM,

00:47:13.910 --> 00:47:16.750 align:middle line:84%
and I choose the
quantity q prime

00:47:16.750 --> 00:47:19.350 align:middle line:84%
that maximizes my
flow payoff, this

00:47:19.350 --> 00:47:21.723 align:middle line:90%
is the payoff I'm going to get.

00:47:21.723 --> 00:47:23.390 align:middle line:84%
And then if I do that,
what state are we

00:47:23.390 --> 00:47:24.410 align:middle line:90%
going to be in tomorrow?

00:47:24.410 --> 00:47:27.870 align:middle line:90%


00:47:27.870 --> 00:47:29.250 align:middle line:90%
Well, we're in this state.

00:47:29.250 --> 00:47:33.910 align:middle line:84%
I deviate, which means we move
to the Nash equilibrium state.

00:47:33.910 --> 00:47:36.230 align:middle line:84%
So from tomorrow
onward, we're going

00:47:36.230 --> 00:47:37.850 align:middle line:84%
to be in the Nash
equilibrium state.

00:47:37.850 --> 00:47:39.670 align:middle line:90%
So my payoff is going to be vNE.

00:47:39.670 --> 00:47:42.110 align:middle line:84%
But the only thing we're
missing is discounting.

00:47:42.110 --> 00:47:44.350 align:middle line:84%
Remember, with average
discounted payoffs,

00:47:44.350 --> 00:47:46.550 align:middle line:84%
how much weight do I
put on what I get today,

00:47:46.550 --> 00:47:49.150 align:middle line:84%
and how much weight do I
put on the infinite future?

00:47:49.150 --> 00:47:52.630 align:middle line:84%
Well, we know we always put
1 minus delta today and delta

00:47:52.630 --> 00:47:53.550 align:middle line:90%
in the future.

00:47:53.550 --> 00:47:59.030 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to have 1 minus
delta here plus delta vNE.

00:47:59.030 --> 00:48:01.910 align:middle line:84%
And it's really important that
we interpret this correctly.

00:48:01.910 --> 00:48:05.270 align:middle line:84%
The first term is what's
the payoff I get today

00:48:05.270 --> 00:48:08.310 align:middle line:84%
if I choose this
optimal deviation?

00:48:08.310 --> 00:48:11.070 align:middle line:84%
This term is not saying,
what's my payoff tomorrow?

00:48:11.070 --> 00:48:14.130 align:middle line:84%
This is incorporating my
payoff tomorrow, the next day,

00:48:14.130 --> 00:48:15.790 align:middle line:90%
the next day, and the next day.

00:48:15.790 --> 00:48:18.750 align:middle line:84%
But by our definition
of vNE, we've

00:48:18.750 --> 00:48:21.510 align:middle line:84%
defined vNE to say, what
is my payoff from today

00:48:21.510 --> 00:48:23.870 align:middle line:90%
onwards once I'm in state NE?

00:48:23.870 --> 00:48:26.950 align:middle line:84%
And that's exactly what we're
going to measure on this side.

00:48:26.950 --> 00:48:28.830 align:middle line:84%
So this is reflect--
it's a little tricky

00:48:28.830 --> 00:48:31.530 align:middle line:84%
because in this case, it happens
to agree with my flow payoff.

00:48:31.530 --> 00:48:33.072 align:middle line:84%
But it's just
important to understand

00:48:33.072 --> 00:48:35.810 align:middle line:84%
that this is representing
everything from tomorrow onward.

00:48:35.810 --> 00:48:38.430 align:middle line:84%
It's not just representing
what happens tomorrow.

00:48:38.430 --> 00:48:40.670 align:middle line:84%
Because what I do today
affects everything

00:48:40.670 --> 00:48:41.910 align:middle line:90%
from tomorrow onwards.

00:48:41.910 --> 00:48:44.870 align:middle line:90%
And now let's go to vNE.

00:48:44.870 --> 00:48:46.690 align:middle line:84%
We're going to have
a similar formula.

00:48:46.690 --> 00:48:51.390 align:middle line:84%
What's our formula
going to be down here?

00:48:51.390 --> 00:48:54.010 align:middle line:84%
Well, I have the same
optimal deviation.

00:48:54.010 --> 00:48:59.590 align:middle line:84%
If I'm going to deviate, I
might as well do the best thing.

00:48:59.590 --> 00:49:02.450 align:middle line:84%
So it's a little
tricky in this case.

00:49:02.450 --> 00:49:06.090 align:middle line:90%


00:49:06.090 --> 00:49:06.590 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:49:06.590 --> 00:49:07.610 align:middle line:90%
So maybe--

00:49:07.610 --> 00:49:15.132 align:middle line:90%


00:49:15.132 --> 00:49:16.590 align:middle line:84%
Here, it's actually
a little tricky

00:49:16.590 --> 00:49:20.630 align:middle line:84%
because my best deviation since
we're in a Nash equilibrium

00:49:20.630 --> 00:49:23.830 align:middle line:84%
is actually to do what
I'm supposed to do.

00:49:23.830 --> 00:49:25.790 align:middle line:84%
But it actually
doesn't matter here

00:49:25.790 --> 00:49:28.630 align:middle line:84%
because we're going
to stay in NE anyway.

00:49:28.630 --> 00:49:32.310 align:middle line:90%
So let's see.

00:49:32.310 --> 00:49:35.830 align:middle line:84%
Maybe this case,
we should just--

00:49:35.830 --> 00:49:37.870 align:middle line:84%
yeah, I feel like this
equation is misleading.

00:49:37.870 --> 00:49:39.610 align:middle line:90%
It's true, but let's just say--

00:49:39.610 --> 00:49:42.990 align:middle line:90%


00:49:42.990 --> 00:49:48.710 align:middle line:84%
I'll just say best
deviation, but my claim

00:49:48.710 --> 00:49:58.910 align:middle line:84%
is that this is never profitable
because if we're in the Nash

00:49:58.910 --> 00:50:02.337 align:middle line:84%
equilibrium state today,
whatever we do today,

00:50:02.337 --> 00:50:04.670 align:middle line:84%
it can't be any better than
playing the Nash equilibrium

00:50:04.670 --> 00:50:07.470 align:middle line:84%
action, and it has no effect
on what happens tomorrow.

00:50:07.470 --> 00:50:08.230 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:50:08.230 --> 00:50:09.890 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: I'm a little confused.

00:50:09.890 --> 00:50:11.690 align:middle line:84%
You said how you define
the value function,

00:50:11.690 --> 00:50:13.150 align:middle line:90%
it's just equal to f.

00:50:13.150 --> 00:50:16.410 align:middle line:84%
And as I understand, f
is just my payoff today.

00:50:16.410 --> 00:50:17.990 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Ah.

00:50:17.990 --> 00:50:18.572 align:middle line:90%
Great point.

00:50:18.572 --> 00:50:19.530 align:middle line:90%
That is very confusing.

00:50:19.530 --> 00:50:20.822 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, I see where you're going.

00:50:20.822 --> 00:50:22.640 align:middle line:84%
It happens to agree
with f, but that's

00:50:22.640 --> 00:50:24.890 align:middle line:84%
simply because we're using
average discounted payoffs.

00:50:24.890 --> 00:50:40.550 align:middle line:84%
So maybe what I should write is
1 minus delta times f of qM--

00:50:40.550 --> 00:50:42.470 align:middle line:90%
let me get at good point--

00:50:42.470 --> 00:50:47.810 align:middle line:84%
plus delta f of qM,
plus dot, dot, dot.

00:50:47.810 --> 00:50:50.350 align:middle line:90%
That's really what I mean.

00:50:50.350 --> 00:50:51.950 align:middle line:90%
Good point.

00:50:51.950 --> 00:50:54.310 align:middle line:84%
So if I want to
compute what happens,

00:50:54.310 --> 00:50:58.570 align:middle line:84%
I'm starting in M. I'm
going to get f of qM today.

00:50:58.570 --> 00:51:00.790 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to get f of
qM tomorrow and so on.

00:51:00.790 --> 00:51:03.990 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to discount, and
I'm going to take the average.

00:51:03.990 --> 00:51:10.050 align:middle line:84%
It turns out that equals f of
qM by the way we've defined it.

00:51:10.050 --> 00:51:10.787 align:middle line:90%
So you're right.

00:51:10.787 --> 00:51:13.370 align:middle line:84%
It's tricky in this case because
it happens to reduce to this.

00:51:13.370 --> 00:51:15.848 align:middle line:84%
But this is the interpretation
of where that's coming from.

00:51:15.848 --> 00:51:16.890 align:middle line:90%
And that reduces to this.

00:51:16.890 --> 00:51:18.090 align:middle line:90%
Is that clear?

00:51:18.090 --> 00:51:18.850 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, great.

00:51:18.850 --> 00:51:19.350 align:middle line:90%
Thanks.

00:51:19.350 --> 00:51:21.392 align:middle line:84%
I should have written it
that way the first time.

00:51:21.392 --> 00:51:23.250 align:middle line:84%
And of course, the
same applies here.

00:51:23.250 --> 00:51:23.750 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:51:23.750 --> 00:51:25.550 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: For the
Nash equilibrium,

00:51:25.550 --> 00:51:28.650 align:middle line:84%
you just said vNE is [INAUDIBLE]
[? never ?] profitable.

00:51:28.650 --> 00:51:33.578 align:middle line:84%
Is that just by definition that
we are in a Nash equilibrium?

00:51:33.578 --> 00:51:35.370 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: It's a little
trickier than that.

00:51:35.370 --> 00:51:37.967 align:middle line:90%
So I'm hesitant to write.

00:51:37.967 --> 00:51:40.050 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I should go back and
write it the other way.

00:51:40.050 --> 00:51:51.030 align:middle line:84%
What's tricky is it's the fact
that we're in a Nash equilibrium

00:51:51.030 --> 00:51:54.673 align:middle line:84%
and that tomorrow
we're going to be

00:51:54.673 --> 00:51:56.090 align:middle line:84%
in the same state,
no matter what.

00:51:56.090 --> 00:51:58.690 align:middle line:84%
So maybe let me write
it out more formally.

00:51:58.690 --> 00:52:00.130 align:middle line:90%
So what we need to show--

00:52:00.130 --> 00:52:03.632 align:middle line:90%


00:52:03.632 --> 00:52:05.590 align:middle line:84%
so in the first case, I
can use this function g

00:52:05.590 --> 00:52:06.490 align:middle line:90%
to simplify my life.

00:52:06.490 --> 00:52:07.698 align:middle line:90%
Here, it's a little trickier.

00:52:07.698 --> 00:52:14.490 align:middle line:84%
So really what I need is it's
greater than pi qi prime.

00:52:14.490 --> 00:52:30.198 align:middle line:90%


00:52:30.198 --> 00:52:31.490 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, let me write it this way.

00:52:31.490 --> 00:52:32.890 align:middle line:84%
And actually, the first one
we could write this way,

00:52:32.890 --> 00:52:33.650 align:middle line:90%
but it reduces.

00:52:33.650 --> 00:52:36.090 align:middle line:90%
So I'm firm i.

00:52:36.090 --> 00:52:39.750 align:middle line:84%
What I could do instead of
following my equilibrium

00:52:39.750 --> 00:52:43.670 align:middle line:84%
strategy is I could instead
produce qi prime today.

00:52:43.670 --> 00:52:46.110 align:middle line:84%
If I produce qi prime
today, and everyone else

00:52:46.110 --> 00:52:48.370 align:middle line:84%
is producing the Nash
equilibrium amount,

00:52:48.370 --> 00:52:50.270 align:middle line:84%
then this is my
flow payoff today.

00:52:50.270 --> 00:52:53.030 align:middle line:84%
And we multiply that
by 1 minus delta.

00:52:53.030 --> 00:52:56.390 align:middle line:84%
Whatever I do today, we're
going to stay in the state Nash

00:52:56.390 --> 00:52:57.330 align:middle line:90%
equilibrium.

00:52:57.330 --> 00:53:00.910 align:middle line:84%
Because if we look at the
arrows here, whatever I do,

00:53:00.910 --> 00:53:03.370 align:middle line:84%
the arrow brings us back to
the Nash equilibrium state.

00:53:03.370 --> 00:53:06.050 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to get delta
and Nash equilibrium tomorrow.

00:53:06.050 --> 00:53:09.590 align:middle line:90%


00:53:09.590 --> 00:53:12.670 align:middle line:84%
And how do we see that this
is less than or equal to this?

00:53:12.670 --> 00:53:16.368 align:middle line:84%
Well, whatever qi
prime might choose,

00:53:16.368 --> 00:53:17.910 align:middle line:84%
it can't give me a
higher flow payoff

00:53:17.910 --> 00:53:19.470 align:middle line:84%
than choosing the
Nash equilibrium

00:53:19.470 --> 00:53:22.710 align:middle line:84%
quantity because that's the
definition of a best response.

00:53:22.710 --> 00:53:30.350 align:middle line:84%
So we see that this is less
than or equal to 1 minus delta f

00:53:30.350 --> 00:53:35.550 align:middle line:90%
of qNE plus delta vNE.

00:53:35.550 --> 00:53:39.430 align:middle line:90%
And that's exactly vNE.

00:53:39.430 --> 00:53:41.870 align:middle line:84%
So how do we see that
this right-hand side

00:53:41.870 --> 00:53:43.330 align:middle line:90%
is smaller than what we want?

00:53:43.330 --> 00:53:46.390 align:middle line:84%
Well, all I've done is I've
compared the flow payoff

00:53:46.390 --> 00:53:47.950 align:middle line:90%
to the Nash equilibrium payoff.

00:53:47.950 --> 00:53:50.570 align:middle line:84%
Whatever quantity I choose,
it can't be as good.

00:53:50.570 --> 00:53:53.390 align:middle line:84%
It's no better than playing my
best response, which is exactly

00:53:53.390 --> 00:53:57.630 align:middle line:84%
the Nash equilibrium quantity,
and that gives me these payoffs.

00:53:57.630 --> 00:54:01.830 align:middle line:84%
And then I substitute
in this over here,

00:54:01.830 --> 00:54:03.990 align:middle line:90%
and we're going to get this.

00:54:03.990 --> 00:54:07.290 align:middle line:90%
So any questions on that?

00:54:07.290 --> 00:54:10.550 align:middle line:90%


00:54:10.550 --> 00:54:12.210 align:middle line:84%
So the action is
going to be here.

00:54:12.210 --> 00:54:15.790 align:middle line:90%


00:54:15.790 --> 00:54:18.790 align:middle line:84%
We want to check that
star is satisfied,

00:54:18.790 --> 00:54:22.970 align:middle line:84%
and let's plug things
in and see what we get.

00:54:22.970 --> 00:54:51.190 align:middle line:90%


00:54:51.190 --> 00:54:53.710 align:middle line:90%
So let's look at star.

00:54:53.710 --> 00:54:55.910 align:middle line:84%
Well, we have our
inequality, star.

00:54:55.910 --> 00:54:58.575 align:middle line:84%
And we have our values for
the v's on the left side.

00:54:58.575 --> 00:54:59.950 align:middle line:84%
So we're just
going to substitute

00:54:59.950 --> 00:55:01.950 align:middle line:84%
these values in to our
inequality over here

00:55:01.950 --> 00:55:02.890 align:middle line:90%
and see what we get.

00:55:02.890 --> 00:55:05.370 align:middle line:90%
So vM becomes f of qM.

00:55:05.370 --> 00:55:08.590 align:middle line:90%


00:55:08.590 --> 00:55:12.750 align:middle line:84%
And that needs to be
greater than or equal to 1

00:55:12.750 --> 00:55:20.910 align:middle line:84%
minus delta g of qM plus delta
times vNE, which we look over

00:55:20.910 --> 00:55:22.250 align:middle line:90%
here, that's f of qM.

00:55:22.250 --> 00:55:28.005 align:middle line:90%


00:55:28.005 --> 00:55:29.630 align:middle line:84%
And here, I think we
can really capture

00:55:29.630 --> 00:55:32.510 align:middle line:84%
exactly the idea of the
temptation of the deviation.

00:55:32.510 --> 00:55:36.350 align:middle line:84%
If I don't deviate, I get
f of qM forever after.

00:55:36.350 --> 00:55:40.910 align:middle line:84%
If I do deviate, I get maybe a
higher number today because I

00:55:40.910 --> 00:55:42.990 align:middle line:90%
increase my flow payoff today.

00:55:42.990 --> 00:55:45.510 align:middle line:84%
But then forever after,
I get a lower number.

00:55:45.510 --> 00:55:53.950 align:middle line:84%
So let's note that g of qM
is strictly greater than f

00:55:53.950 --> 00:55:58.570 align:middle line:84%
of qM, which is, in turn,
strictly greater than f of qNE.

00:55:58.570 --> 00:56:01.550 align:middle line:90%


00:56:01.550 --> 00:56:03.190 align:middle line:84%
So this is what I'm
supposed to get.

00:56:03.190 --> 00:56:06.830 align:middle line:84%
If I deviate, I bump up
my payoff to this today,

00:56:06.830 --> 00:56:09.310 align:middle line:84%
but then I drop down
to this forever after.

00:56:09.310 --> 00:56:11.310 align:middle line:84%
And we want to make sure
that I'm patient enough

00:56:11.310 --> 00:56:14.390 align:middle line:84%
that the dropdown forever
after offsets, or sufficiently

00:56:14.390 --> 00:56:16.210 align:middle line:84%
offsets the
potential game today.

00:56:16.210 --> 00:56:18.790 align:middle line:90%


00:56:18.790 --> 00:56:20.110 align:middle line:90%
Any questions?

00:56:20.110 --> 00:56:29.670 align:middle line:90%


00:56:29.670 --> 00:56:34.950 align:middle line:84%
So now let's just
do some algebra.

00:56:34.950 --> 00:56:42.170 align:middle line:84%
Let's bring-- we want to bring
everything to this side I think.

00:56:42.170 --> 00:56:45.410 align:middle line:84%
So what we're going
to have is delta--

00:56:45.410 --> 00:56:48.480 align:middle line:90%


00:56:48.480 --> 00:56:50.230 align:middle line:84%
I want to bring
everything involving delta

00:56:50.230 --> 00:56:52.397 align:middle line:84%
to the left side, and
everything not involving delta

00:56:52.397 --> 00:56:53.390 align:middle line:90%
to the right side.

00:56:53.390 --> 00:56:55.790 align:middle line:90%
So I have minus delta g of qM.

00:56:55.790 --> 00:56:58.510 align:middle line:84%
I'll bring that to the left
side, and that becomes plus.

00:56:58.510 --> 00:57:00.990 align:middle line:90%
Then I have plus delta f of qNE.

00:57:00.990 --> 00:57:02.890 align:middle line:84%
I'll subtract that and
bring it to this side.

00:57:02.890 --> 00:57:09.090 align:middle line:84%
So I get minus f of qNE
greater than or equal to--

00:57:09.090 --> 00:57:20.710 align:middle line:90%


00:57:20.710 --> 00:57:25.230 align:middle line:84%
So now let me just divide, and
I just get delta greater than

00:57:25.230 --> 00:57:27.470 align:middle line:84%
or equal to this
threshold, maybe

00:57:27.470 --> 00:57:33.110 align:middle line:84%
delta upper bar, which is
defined to equal g of qM

00:57:33.110 --> 00:57:34.650 align:middle line:90%
minus f of qM.

00:57:34.650 --> 00:57:50.070 align:middle line:90%


00:57:50.070 --> 00:57:55.110 align:middle line:84%
And I sometimes think it's
easier to decompose it this way.

00:57:55.110 --> 00:57:56.330 align:middle line:90%
This is g of--

00:57:56.330 --> 00:58:31.310 align:middle line:90%


00:58:31.310 --> 00:58:34.590 align:middle line:84%
So as we expected, this is going
to be a subgame perfect Nash

00:58:34.590 --> 00:58:36.670 align:middle line:84%
equilibrium if delta
is large enough,

00:58:36.670 --> 00:58:38.550 align:middle line:84%
if the players are
patient enough.

00:58:38.550 --> 00:58:44.350 align:middle line:84%
And let's try to interpret
the payoffs over here.

00:58:44.350 --> 00:58:46.590 align:middle line:84%
Well, what I want
to think is I want

00:58:46.590 --> 00:58:50.830 align:middle line:84%
to think that this gap
represents my one-shot gain

00:58:50.830 --> 00:58:51.870 align:middle line:90%
from deviating.

00:58:51.870 --> 00:58:56.870 align:middle line:90%


00:58:56.870 --> 00:59:02.530 align:middle line:84%
But this gap-- this is my
gain today from deviating,

00:59:02.530 --> 00:59:04.850 align:middle line:84%
And this is my
loss forever after.

00:59:04.850 --> 00:59:07.950 align:middle line:90%


00:59:07.950 --> 00:59:11.790 align:middle line:84%
Maybe we'll call this g equals
gain, and this loss equals l.

00:59:11.790 --> 00:59:14.950 align:middle line:84%
So we're supposed to
be playing the monopoly

00:59:14.950 --> 00:59:16.250 align:middle line:90%
quantity every period.

00:59:16.250 --> 00:59:18.630 align:middle line:90%
If I deviate, I can overproduce.

00:59:18.630 --> 00:59:22.110 align:middle line:84%
I do strictly better
today, I gain g for myself.

00:59:22.110 --> 00:59:25.590 align:middle line:84%
But forever after, instead of
getting the monopoly quantity,

00:59:25.590 --> 00:59:27.490 align:middle line:84%
we all produce the Nash
equilibrium quantity,

00:59:27.490 --> 00:59:28.490 align:middle line:90%
which is strictly worse.

00:59:28.490 --> 00:59:32.470 align:middle line:90%
So we lose this amount l.

00:59:32.470 --> 00:59:34.350 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out,
if you just look

00:59:34.350 --> 00:59:37.630 align:middle line:84%
at the algebra I've done
here, g of qM minus f

00:59:37.630 --> 00:59:39.810 align:middle line:90%
of qM, that's exactly the gain.

00:59:39.810 --> 00:59:42.110 align:middle line:90%
So we get g over here.

00:59:42.110 --> 00:59:45.610 align:middle line:84%
And at the bottom, we exactly
have the gain plus the loss.

00:59:45.610 --> 00:59:49.390 align:middle line:90%


00:59:49.390 --> 00:59:52.830 align:middle line:84%
And this is kind of a
general formula that says,

00:59:52.830 --> 00:59:54.750 align:middle line:90%
what's the requirement on delta?

00:59:54.750 --> 00:59:56.950 align:middle line:84%
It has to be large
enough, roughly,

00:59:56.950 --> 01:00:01.470 align:middle line:84%
that the potential
one period gain I get

01:00:01.470 --> 01:00:04.830 align:middle line:84%
is offset by the eventual
loss I experience.

01:00:04.830 --> 01:00:08.898 align:middle line:84%
So let's make sure we understand
how this varies with g and l.

01:00:08.898 --> 01:00:09.690 align:middle line:90%
So let me ask this.

01:00:09.690 --> 01:00:13.170 align:middle line:84%
So if g gets bigger, what
happens to delta bar?

01:00:13.170 --> 01:00:16.820 align:middle line:90%


01:00:16.820 --> 01:00:18.570 align:middle line:84%
Let's start with L.
That's the easier one.

01:00:18.570 --> 01:00:21.950 align:middle line:90%
If L gets bigger what happens?

01:00:21.950 --> 01:00:22.657 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:00:22.657 --> 01:00:23.990 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: I'm going to be honest.

01:00:23.990 --> 01:00:25.710 align:middle line:84%
I'm having trouble
working out the math.

01:00:25.710 --> 01:00:27.550 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: But yeah.

01:00:27.550 --> 01:00:28.718 align:middle line:90%
So this is just algebra.

01:00:28.718 --> 01:00:30.010 align:middle line:90%
You can check that on your own.

01:00:30.010 --> 01:00:34.030 align:middle line:84%
Let's just look at this and this
formula over here, just g over g

01:00:34.030 --> 01:00:34.910 align:middle line:90%
plus l.

01:00:34.910 --> 01:00:36.470 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: As the
loss gets bigger,

01:00:36.470 --> 01:00:38.035 align:middle line:84%
delta is going to
have to be smaller.

01:00:38.035 --> 01:00:39.410 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Delta
bar gets smaller.

01:00:39.410 --> 01:00:41.108 align:middle line:90%
And what is that?

01:00:41.108 --> 01:00:42.150 align:middle line:90%
So can we interpret that?

01:00:42.150 --> 01:00:44.410 align:middle line:84%
As the loss gets bigger,
delta bar gets smaller.

01:00:44.410 --> 01:00:45.302 align:middle line:90%
So what's the--

01:00:45.302 --> 01:00:47.510 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: That just means
you have to put more value--

01:00:47.510 --> 01:00:50.790 align:middle line:84%
or put less value on the future
if you're going to be moving

01:00:50.790 --> 01:00:51.723 align:middle line:90%
more in the future.

01:00:51.723 --> 01:00:52.390 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Right.

01:00:52.390 --> 01:00:54.050 align:middle line:84%
So it's easier to
sustain equilibrium.

01:00:54.050 --> 01:00:56.550 align:middle line:84%
If the loss gets larger,
that means the punishment

01:00:56.550 --> 01:00:57.990 align:middle line:90%
is very powerful.

01:00:57.990 --> 01:01:00.230 align:middle line:84%
If I get caught for
deviating, tomorrow I

01:01:00.230 --> 01:01:01.650 align:middle line:90%
experience this huge loss.

01:01:01.650 --> 01:01:03.630 align:middle line:84%
If I experience a huge
loss for deviating,

01:01:03.630 --> 01:01:05.490 align:middle line:84%
it's not very attractive
for me to deviate.

01:01:05.490 --> 01:01:07.750 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, it's easier to
sustain the equilibrium.

01:01:07.750 --> 01:01:09.870 align:middle line:84%
Because if delta
bar is smaller, that

01:01:09.870 --> 01:01:11.990 align:middle line:84%
means I don't have to
be as patient for this

01:01:11.990 --> 01:01:13.430 align:middle line:90%
to be an equilibrium.

01:01:13.430 --> 01:01:16.150 align:middle line:84%
If l is really, really big, and
the punishment is really, really

01:01:16.150 --> 01:01:18.048 align:middle line:84%
bad, even if I'm
pretty impatient,

01:01:18.048 --> 01:01:19.590 align:middle line:84%
this still constitutes
an equilibrium

01:01:19.590 --> 01:01:22.670 align:middle line:84%
because the punishment
is so severe.

01:01:22.670 --> 01:01:25.830 align:middle line:90%
So more delta satisfy this--

01:01:25.830 --> 01:01:28.230 align:middle line:84%
even smaller deltas,
more impatient deltas,

01:01:28.230 --> 01:01:30.190 align:middle line:90%
when l is very large.

01:01:30.190 --> 01:01:30.930 align:middle line:90%
What about g?

01:01:30.930 --> 01:01:32.252 align:middle line:90%
Can we also reason through g?

01:01:32.252 --> 01:01:33.710 align:middle line:84%
This is a little
trickier because g

01:01:33.710 --> 01:01:37.250 align:middle line:84%
is on the top and the bottom,
but it is increasing in g.

01:01:37.250 --> 01:01:41.190 align:middle line:84%
Maybe it's not quite so
easy to see, but it is.

01:01:41.190 --> 01:01:44.950 align:middle line:90%
So why is this increasing in g?

01:01:44.950 --> 01:01:45.590 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:01:45.590 --> 01:01:47.132 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Well, if
you're able to gain

01:01:47.132 --> 01:01:49.270 align:middle line:84%
more than the
threshold for deciding

01:01:49.270 --> 01:01:51.703 align:middle line:84%
that you shouldn't deviate
would need to be higher.

01:01:51.703 --> 01:01:52.370 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Right.

01:01:52.370 --> 01:01:55.750 align:middle line:84%
If I can gain a lot today,
it's going to be really, really

01:01:55.750 --> 01:01:57.230 align:middle line:90%
tempting for me to deviate.

01:01:57.230 --> 01:01:59.710 align:middle line:84%
So it's going to be harder
to sustain the equilibrium.

01:01:59.710 --> 01:02:01.810 align:middle line:84%
And that's only going to work if
the players are really, really,

01:02:01.810 --> 01:02:02.750 align:middle line:90%
really patient.

01:02:02.750 --> 01:02:06.510 align:middle line:84%
So this threshold gets higher,
meaning fewer deltas satisfy it

01:02:06.510 --> 01:02:10.870 align:middle line:84%
because the temptation to
deviate gets really, really big.

01:02:10.870 --> 01:02:13.150 align:middle line:90%
And you can check that--

01:02:13.150 --> 01:02:14.870 align:middle line:84%
everything's in
terms of f and g.

01:02:14.870 --> 01:02:18.057 align:middle line:84%
But remember, at the end of
the day, f and g involve n.

01:02:18.057 --> 01:02:20.390 align:middle line:84%
So let's just see if we can
get some intuition for this.

01:02:20.390 --> 01:02:21.890 align:middle line:84%
Well, maybe this
is too hard to see,

01:02:21.890 --> 01:02:24.990 align:middle line:84%
but it turns out that
delta bar, you can check,

01:02:24.990 --> 01:02:28.430 align:middle line:90%
is increasing in n.

01:02:28.430 --> 01:02:30.290 align:middle line:84%
So can we give an
economic story?

01:02:30.290 --> 01:02:34.110 align:middle line:90%
What does this tell us?

01:02:34.110 --> 01:02:36.990 align:middle line:84%
Or what might we expect about
which cartels are successful

01:02:36.990 --> 01:02:40.428 align:middle line:84%
and which cartels are not
successful based on this fact?

01:02:40.428 --> 01:02:41.470 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Smaller cartels.

01:02:41.470 --> 01:02:43.338 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Smaller
cartels meaning what?

01:02:43.338 --> 01:02:44.130 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, you're right.

01:02:44.130 --> 01:02:44.590 align:middle line:90%
But--

01:02:44.590 --> 01:02:46.790 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Like the ones that
have less players involved.

01:02:46.790 --> 01:02:47.790 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Fewer firms, right?

01:02:47.790 --> 01:02:48.890 align:middle line:90%
The players themselves are big.

01:02:48.890 --> 01:02:50.290 align:middle line:84%
So we have to be careful
about what small means.

01:02:50.290 --> 01:02:53.250 align:middle line:84%
The players are bigger, but the
number of players is smaller.

01:02:53.250 --> 01:02:57.110 align:middle line:84%
So as n gets bigger, we have
to be really, really patient

01:02:57.110 --> 01:02:59.350 align:middle line:90%
to sustain cartel behavior.

01:02:59.350 --> 01:03:02.630 align:middle line:84%
And given that people aren't
necessarily that patient,

01:03:02.630 --> 01:03:03.990 align:middle line:90%
that may not work out.

01:03:03.990 --> 01:03:05.750 align:middle line:84%
So we would expect
that cartels might

01:03:05.750 --> 01:03:09.490 align:middle line:84%
be more successful and
last longer when n is small

01:03:09.490 --> 01:03:11.490 align:middle line:84%
and we just have a few
big players interacting.

01:03:11.490 --> 01:03:12.990 align:middle line:84%
And indeed, that's
pretty consistent

01:03:12.990 --> 01:03:14.830 align:middle line:84%
with the empirical
evidence, that it's

01:03:14.830 --> 01:03:18.710 align:middle line:84%
hard for many, many firms to
coordinate and work together

01:03:18.710 --> 01:03:20.310 align:middle line:90%
and sustain cartel behavior.

01:03:20.310 --> 01:03:22.430 align:middle line:84%
Even when there's few
firms, things do break down.

01:03:22.430 --> 01:03:27.042 align:middle line:84%
So this may seem kind
of just a math story.

01:03:27.042 --> 01:03:28.250 align:middle line:90%
We have the Nash equilibrium.

01:03:28.250 --> 01:03:29.042 align:middle line:90%
We have a monopoly.

01:03:29.042 --> 01:03:32.670 align:middle line:84%
But this is actually exactly
what happened in the 1980s.

01:03:32.670 --> 01:03:35.390 align:middle line:84%
Saudi Arabia got frustrated that
everyone else was overproducing

01:03:35.390 --> 01:03:37.710 align:middle line:84%
a little bit, and they flooded
the market, and the price of oil

01:03:37.710 --> 01:03:38.690 align:middle line:90%
dropped dramatically.

01:03:38.690 --> 01:03:40.770 align:middle line:84%
So we see exactly this
kind of punishment.

01:03:40.770 --> 01:03:43.365 align:middle line:84%
Saudi Arabia said, look, I'm
tired of these other countries

01:03:43.365 --> 01:03:45.490 align:middle line:84%
overproducing relative to
what they're supposed to.

01:03:45.490 --> 01:03:47.573 align:middle line:84%
And they punished everyone
by flooding the market,

01:03:47.573 --> 01:03:48.730 align:middle line:90%
and the price dropped.

01:03:48.730 --> 01:03:52.870 align:middle line:84%
And this had huge
effects globally.

01:03:52.870 --> 01:03:56.150 align:middle line:84%
Let's do one more
equilibrium construction

01:03:56.150 --> 01:03:57.650 align:middle line:90%
that I think just kind of.

01:03:57.650 --> 01:04:00.710 align:middle line:90%


01:04:00.710 --> 01:04:02.030 align:middle line:90%
Illustrates things.

01:04:02.030 --> 01:04:04.530 align:middle line:90%
So let's do a final equilibrium.

01:04:04.530 --> 01:04:07.130 align:middle line:84%
So before, we did a Nash
reversion equilibrium.

01:04:07.130 --> 01:04:09.470 align:middle line:84%
Now we're going to do
what's called a carrot

01:04:09.470 --> 01:04:10.730 align:middle line:90%
and stick equilibrium.

01:04:10.730 --> 01:04:14.070 align:middle line:90%


01:04:14.070 --> 01:04:17.350 align:middle line:84%
And what was distinctive
about this equilibrium

01:04:17.350 --> 01:04:20.510 align:middle line:84%
is once someone deviated, we
reverted to Nash equilibrium

01:04:20.510 --> 01:04:21.605 align:middle line:90%
forever after.

01:04:21.605 --> 01:04:23.730 align:middle line:84%
But if you look at what
happened with Saudi Arabia,

01:04:23.730 --> 01:04:26.790 align:middle line:84%
they didn't just produce a
high amount of oil forever.

01:04:26.790 --> 01:04:28.608 align:middle line:84%
They flooded the
market for a few years,

01:04:28.608 --> 01:04:31.150 align:middle line:84%
and then they eventually brought
their production down again,

01:04:31.150 --> 01:04:32.410 align:middle line:90%
and prices went up again.

01:04:32.410 --> 01:04:34.150 align:middle line:84%
So what we often
see in these cartels

01:04:34.150 --> 01:04:36.650 align:middle line:84%
is punishments of
limited duration.

01:04:36.650 --> 01:04:37.525 align:middle line:90%
Someone overproduces.

01:04:37.525 --> 01:04:39.692 align:middle line:84%
We punish them for a year
or two, or a month or two,

01:04:39.692 --> 01:04:40.330 align:middle line:90%
or whatever.

01:04:40.330 --> 01:04:42.370 align:middle line:84%
And then we get back to the
good thing that we are doing.

01:04:42.370 --> 01:04:44.030 align:middle line:84%
Because ultimately,
that's what we want to do.

01:04:44.030 --> 01:04:45.863 align:middle line:84%
So the question is, can
we have these carrot

01:04:45.863 --> 01:04:46.950 align:middle line:90%
and stick strategies?

01:04:46.950 --> 01:04:48.350 align:middle line:84%
And the way this
is going to work

01:04:48.350 --> 01:04:51.190 align:middle line:84%
is we're going to have
two production levels-- qc

01:04:51.190 --> 01:04:53.230 align:middle line:90%
less than qs.

01:04:53.230 --> 01:04:55.590 align:middle line:84%
So carrot and stick are
generic terms for carrot

01:04:55.590 --> 01:04:57.850 align:middle line:84%
is a reward and stick
is a punishment.

01:04:57.850 --> 01:04:59.610 align:middle line:84%
This is like, I guess,
animals or pets.

01:04:59.610 --> 01:05:00.830 align:middle line:90%
You give it a carrot or you--

01:05:00.830 --> 01:05:02.070 align:middle line:90%
I mean, it's kind of violent.

01:05:02.070 --> 01:05:04.630 align:middle line:90%
But anyway, that's what it is.

01:05:04.630 --> 01:05:05.290 align:middle line:90%
So OK.

01:05:05.290 --> 01:05:07.582 align:middle line:84%
I'll use this-- maybe I
shouldn't use this terminology,

01:05:07.582 --> 01:05:09.370 align:middle line:84%
but I've inherited
this terminology.

01:05:09.370 --> 01:05:11.510 align:middle line:90%
I'll use it.

01:05:11.510 --> 01:05:16.410 align:middle line:84%
So the carrot is the
low production level.

01:05:16.410 --> 01:05:18.390 align:middle line:84%
This is the good
thing that we like.

01:05:18.390 --> 01:05:20.158 align:middle line:84%
The stick is the high
production level.

01:05:20.158 --> 01:05:22.450 align:middle line:84%
So this could be the Nash
equilibrium and the monopoly,

01:05:22.450 --> 01:05:23.170 align:middle line:90%
but it doesn't have to be.

01:05:23.170 --> 01:05:24.710 align:middle line:84%
It's just general
production levels.

01:05:24.710 --> 01:05:28.030 align:middle line:84%
And the idea is we're going
to start with the carrot.

01:05:28.030 --> 01:05:29.750 align:middle line:84%
And if someone
deviates, we're all

01:05:29.750 --> 01:05:32.270 align:middle line:84%
going to produce the stick
level tomorrow, but only

01:05:32.270 --> 01:05:32.952 align:middle line:90%
for one period.

01:05:32.952 --> 01:05:34.910 align:middle line:84%
Then if everyone does
what they're supposed to,

01:05:34.910 --> 01:05:36.910 align:middle line:84%
we come back to
the carrot level.

01:05:36.910 --> 01:05:41.270 align:middle line:84%
So let's try to represent
this as an automaton.

01:05:41.270 --> 01:05:43.610 align:middle line:84%
So let me erase this
automaton and get a new one.

01:05:43.610 --> 01:05:48.350 align:middle line:90%


01:05:48.350 --> 01:05:50.310 align:middle line:84%
So now our automaton
is going to have

01:05:50.310 --> 01:05:57.150 align:middle line:84%
two states-- the carrot state
and the stick state, so c and s.

01:05:57.150 --> 01:05:59.495 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to start
in the carrot state.

01:05:59.495 --> 01:06:00.870 align:middle line:84%
And in the carrot
state, everyone

01:06:00.870 --> 01:06:04.670 align:middle line:84%
produces this low-level
c, which is good.

01:06:04.670 --> 01:06:06.790 align:middle line:84%
And in the stick state
everyone produces

01:06:06.790 --> 01:06:13.390 align:middle line:84%
this high-level qs, which
is not so good for everyone.

01:06:13.390 --> 01:06:14.890 align:middle line:84%
And now we have to
say what happens.

01:06:14.890 --> 01:06:16.150 align:middle line:84%
I think in this
case, the transitions

01:06:16.150 --> 01:06:17.590 align:middle line:90%
are a little more complicated.

01:06:17.590 --> 01:06:19.550 align:middle line:84%
So in the carrot
state, we're going

01:06:19.550 --> 01:06:22.470 align:middle line:84%
to stay in the carrot state if
everyone produces the carrot

01:06:22.470 --> 01:06:24.350 align:middle line:90%
level.

01:06:24.350 --> 01:06:26.270 align:middle line:84%
So we have an
arrow here if what?

01:06:26.270 --> 01:06:31.170 align:middle line:90%
If qi equals qc for all i.

01:06:31.170 --> 01:06:34.710 align:middle line:84%
If everyone does what they're
supposed to, we stay here.

01:06:34.710 --> 01:06:37.330 align:middle line:84%
Otherwise, we go
to the sixth state.

01:06:37.330 --> 01:06:44.270 align:middle line:90%


01:06:44.270 --> 01:06:47.970 align:middle line:84%
But now this is where
it gets a little tricky.

01:06:47.970 --> 01:06:49.030 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

01:06:49.030 --> 01:06:51.642 align:middle line:84%
Can we figure out what
happens in the stick state?

01:06:51.642 --> 01:06:53.350 align:middle line:84%
What happens if we're
in the stick state,

01:06:53.350 --> 01:06:55.250 align:middle line:84%
and everyone produces
the stick quantity?

01:06:55.250 --> 01:06:56.490 align:middle line:90%
What state do we move to?

01:06:56.490 --> 01:06:59.743 align:middle line:90%
Do we stay, or do we move?

01:06:59.743 --> 01:07:00.910 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Move to the carrot.

01:07:00.910 --> 01:07:01.470 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: We move back.

01:07:01.470 --> 01:07:03.890 align:middle line:84%
Because now if everyone does
what they're supposed to--

01:07:03.890 --> 01:07:05.730 align:middle line:90%
OK, we did our punishment.

01:07:05.730 --> 01:07:08.950 align:middle line:84%
We're now ready to get back
to where we were before.

01:07:08.950 --> 01:07:17.010 align:middle line:84%
So now we're going to have this
arrow if qi equals qs for all i.

01:07:17.010 --> 01:07:20.350 align:middle line:90%


01:07:20.350 --> 01:07:24.710 align:middle line:84%
And then what happens if
someone doesn't play the stick?

01:07:24.710 --> 01:07:27.488 align:middle line:90%
What do we do?

01:07:27.488 --> 01:07:29.030 align:middle line:84%
Well, then we stay
in the stick state

01:07:29.030 --> 01:07:30.390 align:middle line:90%
and punish them again tomorrow.

01:07:30.390 --> 01:07:35.390 align:middle line:90%


01:07:35.390 --> 01:07:37.710 align:middle line:84%
So at first this
may look confusing.

01:07:37.710 --> 01:07:40.900 align:middle line:84%
But I think the way to
interpret it is in either state,

01:07:40.900 --> 01:07:42.650 align:middle line:84%
if everyone does what
they're supposed to,

01:07:42.650 --> 01:07:44.870 align:middle line:84%
we move to the carrot
state tomorrow.

01:07:44.870 --> 01:07:47.270 align:middle line:84%
That's this arrow
and this arrow.

01:07:47.270 --> 01:07:49.190 align:middle line:84%
If anyone does what
they're not supposed to,

01:07:49.190 --> 01:07:51.150 align:middle line:84%
we go to the stick
state tomorrow.

01:07:51.150 --> 01:07:52.708 align:middle line:84%
That's this arrow
and this arrow.

01:07:52.708 --> 01:07:54.750 align:middle line:84%
And that's true whether
we're in the carrot state

01:07:54.750 --> 01:07:56.290 align:middle line:90%
today or the stick state today.

01:07:56.290 --> 01:07:59.267 align:middle line:84%
Either way, we go to the
stick state tomorrow.

01:07:59.267 --> 01:08:00.850 align:middle line:84%
I should say when I
write "otherwise,"

01:08:00.850 --> 01:08:02.350 align:middle line:90%
it can be a bit ambiguous.

01:08:02.350 --> 01:08:05.510 align:middle line:84%
Otherwise is referring to
the source of the node.

01:08:05.510 --> 01:08:08.610 align:middle line:84%
So in the c state think
of, we're in this state.

01:08:08.610 --> 01:08:09.870 align:middle line:90%
Look at all the arrows.

01:08:09.870 --> 01:08:12.288 align:middle line:84%
We could either do this,
or otherwise we do this.

01:08:12.288 --> 01:08:14.830 align:middle line:84%
And then similarly, if I'm in
the stick state, if we do this,

01:08:14.830 --> 01:08:15.410 align:middle line:90%
we go here.

01:08:15.410 --> 01:08:16.550 align:middle line:90%
And otherwise, we go here.

01:08:16.550 --> 01:08:18.270 align:middle line:84%
But when you just see
all the otherwises

01:08:18.270 --> 01:08:19.729 align:middle line:84%
you might think, what
are they referring to?

01:08:19.729 --> 01:08:21.396 align:middle line:84%
They're referring to
the state we're in,

01:08:21.396 --> 01:08:23.750 align:middle line:90%
the source of all the arrows.

01:08:23.750 --> 01:08:26.990 align:middle line:84%
So now, I don't want
to do tons of algebra,

01:08:26.990 --> 01:08:29.149 align:middle line:84%
but I just want to
see if we can set up

01:08:29.149 --> 01:08:31.710 align:middle line:84%
the value function and
the equilibrium conditions

01:08:31.710 --> 01:08:32.651 align:middle line:90%
for this equilibrium.

01:08:32.651 --> 01:08:34.109 align:middle line:84%
And then on the
problem set, you'll

01:08:34.109 --> 01:08:35.569 align:middle line:84%
get to actually do
all the algebra.

01:08:35.569 --> 01:08:37.790 align:middle line:84%
But I think here, we'll see the
benefits of this value function

01:08:37.790 --> 01:08:38.590 align:middle line:90%
approach.

01:08:38.590 --> 01:08:53.434 align:middle line:90%


01:08:53.434 --> 01:08:56.149 align:middle line:90%
So there's two steps always.

01:08:56.149 --> 01:08:58.229 align:middle line:84%
On the left side, we
compute the value functions,

01:08:58.229 --> 01:09:01.630 align:middle line:90%
as we did above.

01:09:01.630 --> 01:09:04.170 align:middle line:84%
And the right side, we write
out the equilibrium conditions.

01:09:04.170 --> 01:09:15.149 align:middle line:90%


01:09:15.149 --> 01:09:17.189 align:middle line:84%
If one of these is the
Nash equilibrium level,

01:09:17.189 --> 01:09:18.090 align:middle line:90%
it's a little tricky.

01:09:18.090 --> 01:09:20.430 align:middle line:84%
So maybe just to
simplify my life,

01:09:20.430 --> 01:09:25.090 align:middle line:84%
let's assume that these
are not equal to qNE.

01:09:25.090 --> 01:09:27.590 align:middle line:84%
So the carrot and the stick are
both different from the Nash

01:09:27.590 --> 01:09:30.910 align:middle line:84%
equilibrium level, just it
avoids this kind of issue

01:09:30.910 --> 01:09:33.529 align:middle line:84%
we got before, where the best
deviation is not a deviation.

01:09:33.529 --> 01:09:36.529 align:middle line:84%
And let's just not
worry about that.

01:09:36.529 --> 01:09:39.149 align:middle line:90%


01:09:39.149 --> 01:09:41.710 align:middle line:84%
Now, I want us to
conceptually understand

01:09:41.710 --> 01:09:43.430 align:middle line:90%
what's happening here.

01:09:43.430 --> 01:09:46.069 align:middle line:84%
The left-hand side is nothing
to do with equilibrium.

01:09:46.069 --> 01:09:47.990 align:middle line:84%
The left-hand side
is just saying,

01:09:47.990 --> 01:09:50.830 align:middle line:84%
if this is the strategy we were
to play, what would everyone's

01:09:50.830 --> 01:09:53.990 align:middle line:90%
payoffs be in each state?

01:09:53.990 --> 01:09:55.572 align:middle line:84%
Then using that
information, we go

01:09:55.572 --> 01:09:57.030 align:middle line:84%
to the right side,
which is saying,

01:09:57.030 --> 01:09:59.630 align:middle line:84%
is it actually an equilibrium
for people to play this way?

01:09:59.630 --> 01:10:02.790 align:middle line:84%
But notice, we can ask, what
would my payoff be in each state

01:10:02.790 --> 01:10:04.470 align:middle line:90%
if this is the strategy we play?

01:10:04.470 --> 01:10:07.190 align:middle line:84%
I can ask that question whether
or not that strategy is actually

01:10:07.190 --> 01:10:08.670 align:middle line:90%
in equilibrium.

01:10:08.670 --> 01:10:11.430 align:middle line:84%
So let's try to write down our
value function formulas here.

01:10:11.430 --> 01:10:18.710 align:middle line:84%
So we have Vc and Vs. So we
have a carrot state and a stick

01:10:18.710 --> 01:10:19.590 align:middle line:90%
state.

01:10:19.590 --> 01:10:20.610 align:middle line:90%
And we want to compute.

01:10:20.610 --> 01:10:23.910 align:middle line:84%
Remember, the interpretation is
the average discounted payoff

01:10:23.910 --> 01:10:26.990 align:middle line:84%
from today onward if
we're in this state.

01:10:26.990 --> 01:10:29.230 align:middle line:84%
So let's say we're
in the c state.

01:10:29.230 --> 01:10:30.850 align:middle line:84%
What is our payoff
going to be today.

01:10:30.850 --> 01:10:32.453 align:middle line:90%
If we all follow the c state?

01:10:32.453 --> 01:10:34.370 align:middle line:84%
We can use the same
notation that was up here?

01:10:34.370 --> 01:10:39.670 align:middle line:90%


01:10:39.670 --> 01:10:41.850 align:middle line:90%
Well, it's f of qc.

01:10:41.850 --> 01:10:48.830 align:middle line:90%


01:10:48.830 --> 01:10:51.950 align:middle line:84%
And we'll multiply
that by 1 minus delta.

01:10:51.950 --> 01:10:57.170 align:middle line:84%
And then if we follow f of
qc, what happens tomorrow?

01:10:57.170 --> 01:10:59.510 align:middle line:90%
What state are we in?

01:10:59.510 --> 01:11:01.350 align:middle line:90%
We're still in the carrot state.

01:11:01.350 --> 01:11:05.130 align:middle line:90%
So now we get delta v of c.

01:11:05.130 --> 01:11:08.310 align:middle line:90%


01:11:08.310 --> 01:11:11.590 align:middle line:84%
Now, this is the one
that's a bit trickier.

01:11:11.590 --> 01:11:14.150 align:middle line:84%
If we're in the
stick state, today we

01:11:14.150 --> 01:11:20.350 align:middle line:90%
get 1 minus delta f of qs.

01:11:20.350 --> 01:11:23.090 align:middle line:84%
But then what do we get on
the right-hand side here?

01:11:23.090 --> 01:11:28.150 align:middle line:90%


01:11:28.150 --> 01:11:29.090 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:11:29.090 --> 01:11:31.470 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Is it delta qc?

01:11:31.470 --> 01:11:32.330 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly.

01:11:32.330 --> 01:11:34.372 align:middle line:84%
Because if we do what
we're supposed to do today,

01:11:34.372 --> 01:11:36.890 align:middle line:84%
we end up in the carrot
state the following period.

01:11:36.890 --> 01:11:40.790 align:middle line:90%


01:11:40.790 --> 01:11:43.190 align:middle line:84%
This is saying, what is my
average discounted payout

01:11:43.190 --> 01:11:44.850 align:middle line:84%
from today onward
in the stick state?

01:11:44.850 --> 01:11:46.550 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's the
payoff I get today

01:11:46.550 --> 01:11:49.270 align:middle line:84%
plus delta-- we've
got a discount--

01:11:49.270 --> 01:11:52.570 align:middle line:84%
times my average discounted
payoff from tomorrow onwards.

01:11:52.570 --> 01:11:53.110 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

01:11:53.110 --> 01:11:55.270 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: So value function
is assuming that you

01:11:55.270 --> 01:11:56.720 align:middle line:90%
follow the strategy profile.

01:11:56.720 --> 01:11:57.470 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly.

01:11:57.470 --> 01:11:58.610 align:middle line:84%
So that's the key
thing over here.

01:11:58.610 --> 01:11:59.690 align:middle line:84%
We're not saying
about equilibrium.

01:11:59.690 --> 01:12:00.730 align:middle line:84%
We're not talking
about deviations.

01:12:00.730 --> 01:12:02.990 align:middle line:84%
We're just saying given
the strategy profile,

01:12:02.990 --> 01:12:06.050 align:middle line:84%
let's compute the value
functions in each state.

01:12:06.050 --> 01:12:09.910 align:middle line:90%


01:12:09.910 --> 01:12:11.790 align:middle line:84%
Now in general,
you can see we're

01:12:11.790 --> 01:12:15.313 align:middle line:84%
going to have a system
of equations with however

01:12:15.313 --> 01:12:16.230 align:middle line:90%
many states there are.

01:12:16.230 --> 01:12:17.110 align:middle line:90%
Call it n.

01:12:17.110 --> 01:12:18.830 align:middle line:84%
No, that's the
number of players.

01:12:18.830 --> 01:12:19.950 align:middle line:90%
t-- k.

01:12:19.950 --> 01:12:22.330 align:middle line:84%
If there's k states-- so
I'll write this down here.

01:12:22.330 --> 01:12:26.550 align:middle line:84%
If there's k states, in general,
we have k equations and k

01:12:26.550 --> 01:12:30.590 align:middle line:84%
unknowns because we have to
specify the value in each state,

01:12:30.590 --> 01:12:32.490 align:middle line:84%
and we have a formula
for each of these.

01:12:32.490 --> 01:12:38.990 align:middle line:84%
So we get k states
and k unknowns.

01:12:38.990 --> 01:12:41.955 align:middle line:84%
And, as you know, this is
going to be a linear system.

01:12:41.955 --> 01:12:44.330 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out it will
actually always have a solution.

01:12:44.330 --> 01:12:46.455 align:middle line:84%
That's something that's a
math result you can show.

01:12:46.455 --> 01:12:49.230 align:middle line:84%
So this is always a way that we
can compute what the values are

01:12:49.230 --> 01:12:50.892 align:middle line:90%
going to be in each state.

01:12:50.892 --> 01:12:53.350 align:middle line:84%
Notice that this is kind of--
there's something very clever

01:12:53.350 --> 01:12:54.150 align:middle line:90%
going on.

01:12:54.150 --> 01:12:56.950 align:middle line:84%
Because if I just
started in one state--

01:12:56.950 --> 01:12:58.690 align:middle line:84%
let's suppose I didn't
use this approach,

01:12:58.690 --> 01:13:00.590 align:middle line:84%
and I just wanted
to say-- let's say

01:13:00.590 --> 01:13:03.587 align:middle line:84%
we had a really complicated--
let's say we had 10 states.

01:13:03.587 --> 01:13:06.170 align:middle line:84%
And I wanted to say, what is my
payoff starting in this state?

01:13:06.170 --> 01:13:07.290 align:middle line:84%
Well, I'd have to
keep going through it.

01:13:07.290 --> 01:13:09.450 align:middle line:84%
I'd say, well, I do this
today, then I go to this state.

01:13:09.450 --> 01:13:10.650 align:middle line:84%
Then I do this tomorrow,
I go to this state.

01:13:10.650 --> 01:13:11.210 align:middle line:90%
I go to this state.

01:13:11.210 --> 01:13:12.130 align:middle line:90%
I go to this state.

01:13:12.130 --> 01:13:14.380 align:middle line:84%
You might think I'd have to
compute the whole infinite

01:13:14.380 --> 01:13:17.430 align:middle line:84%
horizon of all the different
states I could pass through.

01:13:17.430 --> 01:13:19.430 align:middle line:84%
What's kind of magical
about this approach

01:13:19.430 --> 01:13:21.710 align:middle line:84%
is we've just reduced
all these computations

01:13:21.710 --> 01:13:23.750 align:middle line:84%
to a single system of
linear equations, which

01:13:23.750 --> 01:13:24.950 align:middle line:90%
is much easier to solve.

01:13:24.950 --> 01:13:27.910 align:middle line:84%
I don't have to explicitly
compute my entire path

01:13:27.910 --> 01:13:29.055 align:middle line:90%
through the automaton.

01:13:29.055 --> 01:13:30.930 align:middle line:84%
I just have to solve
the system of equations.

01:13:30.930 --> 01:13:32.850 align:middle line:84%
And this is related to
some kind of nice math.

01:13:32.850 --> 01:13:33.390 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:13:33.390 --> 01:13:35.950 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Each unknown
corresponds [INAUDIBLE]

01:13:35.950 --> 01:13:37.680 align:middle line:84%
one-to-one correspondence
between states.

01:13:37.680 --> 01:13:38.430 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly.

01:13:38.430 --> 01:13:40.190 align:middle line:84%
So formally, you can
think of v as a value

01:13:40.190 --> 01:13:43.730 align:middle line:84%
function that's defined on the
domain of the set of states.

01:13:43.730 --> 01:13:46.650 align:middle line:84%
So a function needs to specify
a number for every state,

01:13:46.650 --> 01:13:50.588 align:middle line:84%
and that's precisely the value
we get starting in that state.

01:13:50.588 --> 01:13:52.630 align:middle line:84%
And if we solve this-- we
can pretty easily solve

01:13:52.630 --> 01:13:54.922 align:middle line:84%
this first equation just to
make sure this makes sense.

01:13:54.922 --> 01:13:58.710 align:middle line:84%
If we subtract delta
Vc from the right side,

01:13:58.710 --> 01:14:04.230 align:middle line:84%
then we get 1 minus delta Vc
equals 1 minus delta f of qc.

01:14:04.230 --> 01:14:07.190 align:middle line:84%
So that tells us that
Vc equals f of qc.

01:14:07.190 --> 01:14:09.530 align:middle line:90%
Does that seem right?

01:14:09.530 --> 01:14:10.030 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:14:10.030 --> 01:14:12.670 align:middle line:84%
Because if we're in
state c, we never leave.

01:14:12.670 --> 01:14:15.000 align:middle line:84%
So every period we get
f of qc, and that's

01:14:15.000 --> 01:14:16.250 align:middle line:90%
our average discounted payoff.

01:14:16.250 --> 01:14:16.630 align:middle line:90%
Question?

01:14:16.630 --> 01:14:17.190 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:14:17.190 --> 01:14:19.790 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: I mean, because we know
we stayed there-- or for Vc,

01:14:19.790 --> 01:14:23.813 align:middle line:84%
we know we stay in state c
if we do it the first time.

01:14:23.813 --> 01:14:25.230 align:middle line:84%
Would there have
been [INAUDIBLE].

01:14:25.230 --> 01:14:27.670 align:middle line:84%
Like, mathematically, you would
get the same answer [INAUDIBLE]

01:14:27.670 --> 01:14:29.110 align:middle line:90%
instead of writing [INAUDIBLE].

01:14:29.110 --> 01:14:29.830 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: You're right.

01:14:29.830 --> 01:14:30.990 align:middle line:84%
You could have just
jumped-- you're

01:14:30.990 --> 01:14:31.950 align:middle line:84%
saying you can
basically see what

01:14:31.950 --> 01:14:33.430 align:middle line:90%
the payoff is going to be here.

01:14:33.430 --> 01:14:34.013 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Yeah.

01:14:34.013 --> 01:14:37.030 align:middle line:90%
But delta times f of Vc.

01:14:37.030 --> 01:14:38.790 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: You could have, yeah.

01:14:38.790 --> 01:14:41.090 align:middle line:84%
But it's down here where
the benefit comes in.

01:14:41.090 --> 01:14:44.350 align:middle line:84%
Because here, and more
generally, what happens tomorrow

01:14:44.350 --> 01:14:45.790 align:middle line:90%
depends on a third state.

01:14:45.790 --> 01:14:47.873 align:middle line:84%
So this is the general
approach that always works.

01:14:47.873 --> 01:14:48.582 align:middle line:90%
But you're right.

01:14:48.582 --> 01:14:51.290 align:middle line:84%
In this case, if we're in what's
called an absorbing state,

01:14:51.290 --> 01:14:52.990 align:middle line:84%
once we're there, we never
leave, it's pretty easy

01:14:52.990 --> 01:14:54.130 align:middle line:90%
to calculate what happens.

01:14:54.130 --> 01:14:56.130 align:middle line:84%
We don't need to worry about
what happens in any other state.

01:14:56.130 --> 01:14:58.130 align:middle line:84%
But when we have transitions
between the states,

01:14:58.130 --> 01:14:59.290 align:middle line:90%
then it becomes trickier.

01:14:59.290 --> 01:15:00.910 align:middle line:90%
But yeah, exactly right.

01:15:00.910 --> 01:15:03.030 align:middle line:84%
But this will never give
you the wrong answer.

01:15:03.030 --> 01:15:03.910 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:15:03.910 --> 01:15:04.450 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:15:04.450 --> 01:15:06.630 align:middle line:84%
So now let's write down
our equilibrium conditions.

01:15:06.630 --> 01:15:10.987 align:middle line:84%
Once again, we have a
condition for each state.

01:15:10.987 --> 01:15:13.070 align:middle line:84%
But remember, we're actually
capturing a lot here.

01:15:13.070 --> 01:15:14.653 align:middle line:84%
Because in each
state, there are a lot

01:15:14.653 --> 01:15:17.050 align:middle line:84%
of different one-shot
deviations we could use.

01:15:17.050 --> 01:15:19.750 align:middle line:84%
So when we say equilibrium
condition, what we really mean

01:15:19.750 --> 01:15:21.430 align:middle line:84%
is we're saying in
each state we want

01:15:21.430 --> 01:15:25.562 align:middle line:84%
to check that no one-shot
deviation is profitable.

01:15:25.562 --> 01:15:27.270 align:middle line:84%
And the way we can
check that is to check

01:15:27.270 --> 01:15:29.830 align:middle line:84%
that the best one-shot
deviation is not profitable.

01:15:29.830 --> 01:15:32.020 align:middle line:84%
Because if the best
deviation is not profitable,

01:15:32.020 --> 01:15:33.770 align:middle line:84%
then none of the
deviations is profitable.

01:15:33.770 --> 01:15:35.210 align:middle line:84%
There may be some
really bad deviations.

01:15:35.210 --> 01:15:36.250 align:middle line:90%
We don't care about those.

01:15:36.250 --> 01:15:38.290 align:middle line:84%
We only care about the
most attractive deviations.

01:15:38.290 --> 01:15:40.415 align:middle line:84%
And that's exactly what
we're going to put in here.

01:15:40.415 --> 01:15:42.640 align:middle line:84%
So in the carrot state,
we need Vc greater than

01:15:42.640 --> 01:15:44.390 align:middle line:84%
or equal to something,
and Vs greater than

01:15:44.390 --> 01:15:45.310 align:middle line:90%
or equal to something.

01:15:45.310 --> 01:15:46.935 align:middle line:84%
Eventually, we'll
substitute things in.

01:15:46.935 --> 01:15:49.150 align:middle line:84%
But I want to just do it
conceptually this way.

01:15:49.150 --> 01:15:53.990 align:middle line:84%
So in the carrot state,
once again, if we deviate

01:15:53.990 --> 01:15:55.390 align:middle line:90%
we're going to get punished.

01:15:55.390 --> 01:15:57.670 align:middle line:84%
And the punishment we
experience doesn't depend

01:15:57.670 --> 01:15:59.250 align:middle line:90%
on the way in which we deviate.

01:15:59.250 --> 01:16:01.870 align:middle line:84%
So we might as well choose
the deviation that maximizes

01:16:01.870 --> 01:16:03.455 align:middle line:90%
our flow payoff today.

01:16:03.455 --> 01:16:04.830 align:middle line:84%
And what is our
flow payoff going

01:16:04.830 --> 01:16:07.990 align:middle line:84%
to be if we choose the deviation
that maximizes our flow payoff?

01:16:07.990 --> 01:16:09.790 align:middle line:90%
We have a function for this.

01:16:09.790 --> 01:16:11.770 align:middle line:90%
It's g of qc.

01:16:11.770 --> 01:16:17.870 align:middle line:90%


01:16:17.870 --> 01:16:22.390 align:middle line:84%
So this says, if I choose
the best deviation today,

01:16:22.390 --> 01:16:23.630 align:middle line:90%
this is what I get today.

01:16:23.630 --> 01:16:26.730 align:middle line:84%
If I deviate, what state are
we going to be in tomorrow?

01:16:26.730 --> 01:16:30.210 align:middle line:90%


01:16:30.210 --> 01:16:30.710 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:16:30.710 --> 01:16:31.530 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Vc.

01:16:31.530 --> 01:16:32.530 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Yeah, exactly.

01:16:32.530 --> 01:16:35.423 align:middle line:84%
So now we're going to
get the s over here.

01:16:35.423 --> 01:16:37.090 align:middle line:84%
And now I think this
is the hardest one,

01:16:37.090 --> 01:16:39.030 align:middle line:90%
and we'll stop here.

01:16:39.030 --> 01:16:41.030 align:middle line:90%
Now we're in the stick state.

01:16:41.030 --> 01:16:43.690 align:middle line:84%
We're going to choose our best
deviation in the stick state.

01:16:43.690 --> 01:16:48.630 align:middle line:90%


01:16:48.630 --> 01:16:50.790 align:middle line:84%
Again, I'm only allowed
to write it this way

01:16:50.790 --> 01:16:53.110 align:middle line:84%
because the punishment
I experience doesn't

01:16:53.110 --> 01:16:56.230 align:middle line:84%
depend on exactly which
quantity I deviate to.

01:16:56.230 --> 01:16:59.250 align:middle line:84%
If the deviation I chose
affected the punishment,

01:16:59.250 --> 01:17:00.165 align:middle line:90%
then we'd have to--

01:17:00.165 --> 01:17:01.790 align:middle line:84%
things would be a
lot more complicated.

01:17:01.790 --> 01:17:04.610 align:middle line:84%
But all we have to ask
is, am I deviating or not?

01:17:04.610 --> 01:17:07.365 align:middle line:84%
However I deviate, it's treated
the same way in the future.

01:17:07.365 --> 01:17:08.990 align:middle line:84%
So I might as well
choose the deviation

01:17:08.990 --> 01:17:10.530 align:middle line:90%
that maximizes my flow payoff.

01:17:10.530 --> 01:17:13.790 align:middle line:90%


01:17:13.790 --> 01:17:22.310 align:middle line:84%
And then what do we get
on the right-hand side?

01:17:22.310 --> 01:17:23.390 align:middle line:90%
Delta times what?

01:17:23.390 --> 01:17:27.470 align:middle line:90%
What happens if I deviate?

01:17:27.470 --> 01:17:28.130 align:middle line:90%
Vs again.

01:17:28.130 --> 01:17:32.372 align:middle line:90%


01:17:32.372 --> 01:17:35.090 align:middle line:84%
So notice when we
were defining these,

01:17:35.090 --> 01:17:37.097 align:middle line:84%
we always had Vc
here because we were

01:17:37.097 --> 01:17:39.430 align:middle line:84%
looking at what happens if I
do what I'm supposed to do.

01:17:39.430 --> 01:17:41.170 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, we go
to the carrot state.

01:17:41.170 --> 01:17:43.170 align:middle line:84%
When we're looking at the
equilibrium condition,

01:17:43.170 --> 01:17:45.510 align:middle line:84%
we're considering deviations
on the right-hand side.

01:17:45.510 --> 01:17:48.630 align:middle line:84%
Whenever I deviate, regardless
of which state I'm in,

01:17:48.630 --> 01:17:50.830 align:middle line:90%
I go to the stick state.

01:17:50.830 --> 01:18:02.550 align:middle line:90%


01:18:02.550 --> 01:18:06.750 align:middle line:84%
Now, let's just understand what
I think is kind of tricky here,

01:18:06.750 --> 01:18:10.430 align:middle line:84%
is this is actually
capturing a lot.

01:18:10.430 --> 01:18:13.430 align:middle line:90%
If I deviate today--

01:18:13.430 --> 01:18:15.310 align:middle line:84%
so let's just
understand the anatomy

01:18:15.310 --> 01:18:19.430 align:middle line:90%
of my one-shot deviation.

01:18:19.430 --> 01:18:28.050 align:middle line:84%
So we have today, tomorrow,
and then thereafter.

01:18:28.050 --> 01:18:31.270 align:middle line:90%


01:18:31.270 --> 01:18:36.390 align:middle line:84%
If I deviate today to, say,
qi prime in the stick state,

01:18:36.390 --> 01:18:38.743 align:middle line:84%
we had a really simple formula
on the right-hand side.

01:18:38.743 --> 01:18:40.910 align:middle line:84%
But what's actually happening
is pretty complicated.

01:18:40.910 --> 01:18:44.070 align:middle line:84%
If I deviate today
to qi prime and then

01:18:44.070 --> 01:18:46.350 align:middle line:84%
return to my
equilibrium strategy,

01:18:46.350 --> 01:18:48.090 align:middle line:84%
what am I going to
produce tomorrow?

01:18:48.090 --> 01:18:56.870 align:middle line:90%


01:18:56.870 --> 01:18:58.150 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE] want to guess?

01:18:58.150 --> 01:18:59.430 align:middle line:90%
You see it?

01:18:59.430 --> 01:19:02.030 align:middle line:84%
Well, tomorrow I go
to the stick state.

01:19:02.030 --> 01:19:05.030 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to
produce qs tomorrow.

01:19:05.030 --> 01:19:06.950 align:middle line:84%
And then the day after
that, I'm actually

01:19:06.950 --> 01:19:12.473 align:middle line:84%
going to produce thereafter,
I'm going to produce qc.

01:19:12.473 --> 01:19:14.890 align:middle line:84%
So this one-shot deviation is
actually really complicated.

01:19:14.890 --> 01:19:16.150 align:middle line:90%
I'm in the stick state.

01:19:16.150 --> 01:19:17.190 align:middle line:90%
I deviate.

01:19:17.190 --> 01:19:19.070 align:middle line:84%
That means I stay
in the stick state.

01:19:19.070 --> 01:19:21.030 align:middle line:84%
Tomorrow, I'm following
my equilibrium strategy

01:19:21.030 --> 01:19:23.390 align:middle line:84%
in the stick state,
so I choose qs.

01:19:23.390 --> 01:19:25.650 align:middle line:84%
But if I choose qs and
I'm in the stick state,

01:19:25.650 --> 01:19:27.850 align:middle line:84%
then we transition to the
carrot state the next day.

01:19:27.850 --> 01:19:30.070 align:middle line:84%
And then I choose
qc the next day.

01:19:30.070 --> 01:19:32.910 align:middle line:84%
So the path of play
following this deviation--

01:19:32.910 --> 01:19:36.390 align:middle line:84%
and then it's going to
be qc forever after--

01:19:36.390 --> 01:19:38.310 align:middle line:90%
is actually really complicated.

01:19:38.310 --> 01:19:42.230 align:middle line:84%
When we wrote our formula here,
things looked much simpler.

01:19:42.230 --> 01:19:45.150 align:middle line:84%
How is this formula reflecting
this complicated path

01:19:45.150 --> 01:19:46.010 align:middle line:90%
moving forward?

01:19:46.010 --> 01:19:48.410 align:middle line:90%


01:19:48.410 --> 01:19:48.910 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:19:48.910 --> 01:19:50.243 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: I just have a question.

01:19:50.243 --> 01:19:52.830 align:middle line:84%
Why are sometimes s And
c subscripts, other times

01:19:52.830 --> 01:19:56.030 align:middle line:90%
superscripts?

01:19:56.030 --> 01:19:57.710 align:middle line:90%
Good point.

01:19:57.710 --> 01:20:03.170 align:middle line:84%
I think I want these
to be superscripts.

01:20:03.170 --> 01:20:06.868 align:middle line:90%


01:20:06.868 --> 01:20:08.410 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: There's
not a great reason.

01:20:08.410 --> 01:20:11.993 align:middle line:84%
I often had q sub i here, so
I made this a superscript.

01:20:11.993 --> 01:20:14.410 align:middle line:84%
But then I never have v sub
i, so I made this a subscript.

01:20:14.410 --> 01:20:15.730 align:middle line:90%
But everything's the same.

01:20:15.730 --> 01:20:16.230 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:20:16.230 --> 01:20:17.870 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Also, was
qi prime-- that

01:20:17.870 --> 01:20:20.790 align:middle line:84%
could be an either stick
or a carrot state, right?

01:20:20.790 --> 01:20:22.090 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: I'm looking at this--

01:20:22.090 --> 01:20:23.673 align:middle line:84%
I'm looking specifically
at this case.

01:20:23.673 --> 01:20:25.230 align:middle line:90%
I'm in the stick state.

01:20:25.230 --> 01:20:27.270 align:middle line:90%
I deviate.

01:20:27.270 --> 01:20:29.410 align:middle line:84%
Because I deviate, I'm in
the stick state tomorrow.

01:20:29.410 --> 01:20:31.910 align:middle line:84%
So then I follow my strategy
since it's a one-shot deviation

01:20:31.910 --> 01:20:33.490 align:middle line:90%
and choose the stick quantity.

01:20:33.490 --> 01:20:35.750 align:middle line:84%
But then the next period,
I'm in the carrot state.

01:20:35.750 --> 01:20:36.930 align:middle line:90%
And then I choose qc.

01:20:36.930 --> 01:20:39.230 align:middle line:84%
So my point here is that
a one-shot deviation

01:20:39.230 --> 01:20:42.510 align:middle line:84%
can lead to a really
complicated path of play.

01:20:42.510 --> 01:20:45.070 align:middle line:84%
The reason I don't have
to worry about it is Vs

01:20:45.070 --> 01:20:47.390 align:middle line:84%
is already taking into
account this future path.

01:20:47.390 --> 01:20:52.150 align:middle line:84%
So this path here
of SCCC is already

01:20:52.150 --> 01:20:54.870 align:middle line:84%
reflected in the
value function Vs.

01:20:54.870 --> 01:20:57.390 align:middle line:84%
In fact, we can see over
here Vs is precisely

01:20:57.390 --> 01:20:59.310 align:middle line:84%
reflecting that I get
the stick today and then

01:20:59.310 --> 01:21:00.365 align:middle line:90%
the carrot forever after.

01:21:00.365 --> 01:21:02.990 align:middle line:84%
So this is showing you the power
of the value functions, that I

01:21:02.990 --> 01:21:05.673 align:middle line:84%
don't really have to keep track
of the total sequence of play.

01:21:05.673 --> 01:21:08.090 align:middle line:84%
I just have to keep track of
what state I get to tomorrow,

01:21:08.090 --> 01:21:09.465 align:middle line:84%
and then it takes
care of itself.

01:21:09.465 --> 01:21:10.290 align:middle line:90%
